# stdlib.notes -- prose for the generated standard-library topics
# (COMPILERPEDIA.md, "The standard library"). Each entry is a topic-format
# block whose ID is a generated topic's (stdlib.NS::NAME, stdlib.NAME for a
# root primitive, stdlib.module.NS for a namespace); tools/compilerpedia.tcl
# generate merges its summary (which replaces the derived one), aliases,
# keywords, related topics, sections and examples into that topic. Every
# public intrinsic needs one: the registry gives its signature, not what it
# does. Write `index: stdlib` and `title: -` (the generated title is kept).

@topic stdlib.+
title: -
summary: The Int sum of A and B, written a + b: exact at any size, so it never overflows or wraps. On two Float64 operands, a + b is Float64 addition.
index: stdlib
keywords: add; addition; plus; sum; arithmetic
related: concept.integers concept.floats stdlib.- stdlib.* stdlib./ stdlib.std::str::concat capability.integer-division

== NOTES
Written as an operator, a + b: the native's name is +, and +(a, b) is not
source syntax. Both operands must be Ints, and the result is the exact sum
however large it is: Botlish has one Int type, of arbitrary precision, with
no 64-bit overflow, wrapping or saturation.

On two Float64 operands the operator is Float64 addition instead
(concept.floats): the compiler chooses by the operands' static types, an
Int and a Float64 together are rejected (TYPE), and nothing is converted
implicitly. A sum that is not finite fails with NonFiniteFloat.

+ is not overloaded for any other kind. "ab" + "cd" fails when it runs with
the TYPE error `+: expected int, got "ab"`: join Strings with
std::str::concat (or add one character with std::str::append_char), and add
one element to a List with std::list::append. There is no integer division
(capability.integer-division); / divides Float64 values (stdlib./).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
x = 9223372036854775807
[x + 1, 2 + -3]
@value [9223372036854775808, -1]
@caption No overflow past the 64-bit range
@end

@topic stdlib.-
title: -
summary: The Int difference A minus B, written a - b, and -x negates: exact at any size, never wrapping. On Float64 operands both are Float64 operations.
index: stdlib
keywords: subtract; subtraction; minus; difference; negate; negative number
related: concept.integers concept.floats stdlib.+ stdlib.mod

== NOTES
Written as an operator: a - b, and a leading minus negates (-x). Both
operands must be Ints (another kind fails with the TYPE error when the
operation runs), and the result is exact at any magnitude. On two Float64
operands a - b is Float64 subtraction, and -x of a Float64 negates it
(concept.floats); an Int and a Float64 together are rejected (TYPE).

A leading minus binds less tightly than a method call: -7.mod(2) is
-(7.mod(2)), which is -1. To reduce a negative number, bind it first
(x = -7, then x.mod(2)) or write mod(-7, 2).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
x = 5
[x - 8, -x, 0 - x]
@value [-3, -5, -5]
@end

@topic stdlib.*
title: -
summary: The Int product of A and B, written a * b: exact at any size (no overflow). On two Float64 operands, a * b is Float64 multiplication.
index: stdlib
keywords: multiply; multiplication; times; product
related: concept.integers concept.floats stdlib.+ stdlib./ stdlib.shift_left capability.integer-division

== NOTES
Written as an operator, a * b, on two Ints; the product is exact however
many digits it has. On two Float64 operands it is Float64 multiplication
(concept.floats), failing with NonFiniteFloat when the product is not
finite. There is no power operator (`**` is a syntax error) and no
integer division (capability.integer-division); a multiplication by a
power of two can also be written x.shift_left(k).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
[6 * 7, 99999999999 * 99999999999, -4 * 3]
@value [42, 9999999999800000000001, -12]
@end

@topic stdlib./
title: -
summary: The Float64 quotient of A and B, written a / b: Float64s only (there is no integer division); a quotient that is not finite, as after division by zero, fails with NonFiniteFloat.
index: stdlib
keywords: divide; division; quotient; slash; float division; divide by zero
related: concept.floats stdlib.* stdlib.std::float64::from_int stdlib.std::float64::truncate capability.integer-division error.known-error

== NOTES
Written as an operator, a / b, on two Float64 operands, giving the
correctly rounded Float64 quotient. It is the one division of the
language: on two Ints it is rejected at compile time (TYPE: "Botlish has
no integer division"), and an Int and a Float64 together are rejected
too, since nothing converts implicitly; convert with std::float64::from_int.

A quotient that is not a finite number -- any division by zero, and an
overflow past the largest Float64 -- fails with the declared error
NonFiniteFloat, which a handler catches like any declared error. When the
compiler knows both operands (literals, or bindings of them) it divides
while compiling: a decided failure is the KNOWN-ERROR diagnostic, not a
run-time failure.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn inverse(x: Float64) -> Float64:
    1.0 / x:
        on NonFiniteFloat:
            0.0

four = 4.0
zero = 0.0
[7.0 / 2.0, 1.0 / 3.0, four.inverse(), zero.inverse()]
@value [3.5, 0.3333333333333333, 0.25, 0.0]
@caption Division, and a handled division by zero
@end

@example reject
x = 7 / 2
x
@caption No integer division
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 1:5
@end

@topic stdlib.<
title: -
summary: Whether the Int A is less than the Int B, written a < b: Ints, or two Float64s; comparisons do not chain.
index: stdlib
keywords: less than; compare numbers; comparison; ordering
related: concept.integers concept.floats stdlib.<= stdlib.> stdlib.>= stdlib.== proof.ranges proof.list-bounds

== NOTES
Written as an operator, a < b, giving a Bool. Both operands must be Ints,
or both Float64s (concept.floats: the compiler chooses by the operands'
static types, and an Int and a Float64 together are rejected). Strings,
characters, Lists and Bools have no ordering operator, and an operand of
another kind fails with the TYPE error when the comparison runs
(`<: expected int, got "a"`). To order characters, compare their
std::char::scalar_value.

Comparisons do not chain: `a < b < c` is a syntax error ("comparison
chaining is not supported"); write `a < b and b < c`.

The four ordering comparisons are what the range analysis reads: a branch
on `i < xs.length()` or `i >= 0` narrows i inside the branch, which is how
the compiler proves an index in range (proof.list-bounds, proof.ranges).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
x = 3
[x < 5, 5 < x, x < 3]
@value [true, false, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.<=
title: -
summary: Whether the Int A is at most the Int B, written a <= b: Ints, or two Float64s; no chaining.
index: stdlib
keywords: less than or equal; at most; compare numbers; comparison
related: concept.integers stdlib.< stdlib.> stdlib.>= proof.ranges

== NOTES
Written as an operator, a <= b, on two Ints or two Float64s (another
kind is the TYPE error when it runs). Like every comparison it does not
chain and it narrows ranges in the branches of an `if` (see stdlib.<).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
x = 3
[x <= 3, x <= 2]
@value [true, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.>
title: -
summary: Whether the Int A is greater than the Int B, written a > b: Ints, or two Float64s; no chaining.
index: stdlib
keywords: greater than; more than; compare numbers; comparison
related: concept.integers stdlib.< stdlib.<= stdlib.>= proof.ranges

== NOTES
Written as an operator, a > b, on two Ints or two Float64s (another
kind is the TYPE error when it runs). Like every comparison it does not
chain and it narrows ranges in the branches of an `if` (see stdlib.<).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
x = 3
[x > 2, x > 3]
@value [true, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.>=
title: -
summary: Whether the Int A is at least the Int B, written a >= b: Ints, or two Float64s; no chaining.
index: stdlib
keywords: greater than or equal; at least; compare numbers; comparison
related: concept.integers stdlib.< stdlib.<= stdlib.> proof.ranges

== NOTES
Written as an operator, a >= b, on two Ints or two Float64s (another
kind is the TYPE error when it runs). Like every comparison it does not
chain and it narrows ranges in the branches of an `if` (see stdlib.<).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
x = 3
[x >= 3, x >= 4]
@value [true, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.==
title: -
summary: Whether A and B are the same value, written a == b: structural equality, Strings included, never converting between kinds.
index: stdlib
keywords: equal; equality; compare values; same value; string equality; not equal; !=
related: rule.equality stdlib.hash concept.values concept.structs

== NOTES
Written as an operator, a == b; `a != b` means not (a == b). It is the
one equality of the language, for Strings as for everything else (the
old `eq` is gone).

It compares structure: Ints and Float64s numerically, Strings by their
characters (exact Unicode scalar values: no case folding, no
normalization), Lists element by element, structs by shape (the same
declaration, or the same anonymous field set) and then field by field,
enum cases by identity, ImmutableSets as sets (order and duplicates do not
matter), Bytes by their bytes. Values of different kinds are never equal
and nothing is converted: "10" == 10 is false, 1.0 == 1 is false, a
UnicodeChar never equals a String ('a' == "a" is false), and a named
struct never equals an anonymous one with the same fields.

Some values have no equality. Functions (closures and natives),
MutableArray and MutableVector values fail with the EQUALITY error when
the comparison runs. An affine value (a coroutine handle, or a struct or
List that owns one) is rejected at compile time with
AFFINE-EQUALITY-UNSUPPORTED. hash agrees with ==: equal values hash equal.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
struct P:
    x: int

[
    [1, [2]] == [1, [2]],
    "10" == 10,
    P {x: 1} == P {x: 1},
    P {x: 1} == {x: 1},
    1 != 2
]
@value [true, false, true, false, true]
@end

@topic stdlib.mod
title: -
summary: The Euclidean remainder of A divided by B, written mod(a, b) or a.mod(b): always 0 <= r < abs(b); B = 0 fails with ARITHMETIC.
index: stdlib
keywords: modulo; remainder; modulus; divisible; even or odd; wrap around; bucket index
related: concept.integers capability.integer-division stdlib.hash stdlib.shift_right proof.ranges proof.list-bounds

== NOTES
A language primitive: no import, written mod(a, b) or, as a method,
a.mod(b) (the METHOD-ELIGIBLE warning suggests the method form when the
first argument is not a literal). The remainder is Euclidean: it is never
negative, whatever the signs, so mod(-7, 3) is 2 and mod(7, -3) is 1.
That makes it safe for wrapping an index or a hash into a table:
hash(key).mod(capacity) is always a valid slot.

A zero divisor fails with the run-time error ARITHMETIC ("mod: division by
zero"). It is not a declared error, so no `on` handler catches it: test
the divisor first.

There is no integer division (no `/`, no `div`): see
capability.integer-division. The range analysis knows mod's result: with a
divisor of bounded magnitude m it lies in 0..m-1, so `xs.at(k.mod(3))` is
proven in range where xs is known to have three elements; and a divisor that
is the container's own size proves the read whatever that size is,
`xs.at(k.mod(xs.length()))` (proof.list-bounds).

A leading minus binds less tightly than the method call: -7.mod(3) is
-(7.mod(3)), which is -1, not 2.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
x = -7
[x.mod(3), mod(7, -3), mod(10, 5)]
@value [2, 1, 0]
@end

@topic stdlib.bit_and
title: -
summary: The bitwise AND of two Ints, written bit_and(a, b), in two's complement over arbitrary precision.
index: stdlib
keywords: bitwise and; mask; bits; bit mask; &
related: concept.integers stdlib.bit_or stdlib.bit_xor stdlib.shift_right stdlib.module.std::byte

== NOTES
The operands are Ints of any size, read as infinite two's-complement bit
strings, so bit_and(-1, 255) is 255 and the result of a nonnegative mask
is never negative. There is no `&` operator.

bit_and, bit_or and bit_xor are declared nomethod: their two operands are
symmetric, so neither is a receiver, and a.bit_and(b) is rejected with
NOMETHOD-CALL. The range analysis bounds the result (an AND with a
nonnegative mask lies in 0..mask), which is how stdlib/byte.bot's functions
prove their Byte and Nibble results; a mask that is a container's size minus
one, with the size proven not 0, proves an index into it
(`a.at(bit_and(h, a.capacity() - 1))`, proof.list-bounds).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
[bit_and(12, 10), bit_and(-1, 255), bit_and(300, 255)]
@value [8, 255, 44]
@end

@topic stdlib.bit_or
title: -
summary: The bitwise OR of two Ints, written bit_or(a, b), in two's complement over arbitrary precision.
index: stdlib
keywords: bitwise or; set bits; combine flags; bits; |
related: concept.integers stdlib.bit_and stdlib.bit_xor stdlib.shift_left

== NOTES
The operands are Ints of any size, read as infinite two's-complement bit
strings. There is no `|` operator. Declared nomethod, like bit_and: call
it as bit_or(a, b) (a.bit_or(b) is NOMETHOD-CALL).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
[bit_or(12, 10), bit_or(1, 256)]
@value [14, 257]
@end

@topic stdlib.bit_xor
title: -
summary: The bitwise exclusive OR of two Ints, written bit_xor(a, b), in two's complement over arbitrary precision.
index: stdlib
keywords: bitwise xor; exclusive or; flip bits; toggle; bits; ^
related: concept.integers stdlib.bit_and stdlib.bit_or stdlib.module.std::byte

== NOTES
The operands are Ints of any size, read as infinite two's-complement bit
strings: bit_xor(x, -1) is -x - 1, not an 8- or 64-bit complement (for a
Byte's complement see std::byte::complement). There is no `^` operator.
Declared nomethod, like bit_and: call it as bit_xor(a, b).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
[bit_xor(12, 10), bit_xor(5, -1)]
@value [6, -6]
@end

@topic stdlib.shift_left
title: -
summary: The Int X shifted left by K bits, written x.shift_left(k): x times 2 to the power k, exact; K must be 0..1048576.
index: stdlib
keywords: shift left; bit shift; multiply by power of two; <<
related: concept.integers stdlib.shift_right stdlib.* stdlib.bit_or

== NOTES
X is any Int and the result is exactly X * 2^K: nothing is shifted out,
since Ints have no width. A shift amount below 0 or above 1048576 (2^20)
fails with the run-time RANGE error "shift amount out of range
(0..1048576)". There is no `<<` operator.

Unlike the bit_* operations, the shifts take method syntax: the shifted
value is the receiver, x.shift_left(k).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
x = 1
[x.shift_left(4), shift_left(-3, 2), shift_left(1, 70)]
@value [16, -12, 1180591620717411303424]
@end

@topic stdlib.shift_right
title: -
summary: The Int X shifted right by K bits, written x.shift_right(k): the floor of x divided by 2 to the power k; K must be 0..1048576.
index: stdlib
keywords: shift right; bit shift; halve; divide by power of two; >>
related: concept.integers stdlib.shift_left stdlib.bit_and capability.integer-division

== NOTES
An arithmetic shift over arbitrary precision: the result is the floor of
X / 2^K, so a negative X stays negative (shift_right(-9, 1) is -5,
shift_right(-1, 3) is -1). It is the one way to divide by a power of two;
there is no general division (capability.integer-division). A shift
amount below 0 or above 1048576 fails with the run-time RANGE error. There
is no `>>` operator; the method form x.shift_right(k) reads best.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
x = 100
[x.shift_right(3), shift_right(-9, 1), shift_right(-1, 3)]
@value [12, -5, -1]
@end

@topic stdlib.list
title: -
summary: The List of its arguments, in order: what a list literal [a, b, c] calls; list() is the empty List.
index: stdlib
keywords: list literal; make a list; build a list; new list; empty list
related: concept.lists stdlib.module.std::list stdlib.std::list::append

== NOTES
A language primitive taking any number of arguments of any kinds. Source
normally writes the literal `[a, b, c]`, which is a call of this
primitive; `list(a, b, c)` is the same value. A List is immutable: there
is no operation that changes one, only ones that make a new List
(std::list::append, a collecting loop).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
[list(1, 2, 3) == [1, 2, 3], list(), [1, "two", [3]]]
@value [true, [], [1, "two", [3]]]
@end

@topic stdlib.integer?
title: -
summary: Whether a value is an Int; inside `if integer?(x):` the compiler knows x is an Int.
index: stdlib
keywords: is integer; is number; type test; kind test; check type
related: concept.types stdlib.string? stdlib.list? stdlib.mutarray? proof.types

== NOTES
A type test: it answers exactly whether its argument is an Int, never fails
and allocates nothing, and the branch it guards knows the type, so an
untyped parameter can be used as an Int there. An integer domain's value (a
std::byte::Byte, ...) is an Int too. There is no type test for Bools,
characters, structs or enum values, and no type gets one by being declared:
the kind tests are integer?, string?, list?, mutarray?, ok? and error?
(rule.no-callable-types).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn double(x) -> int:
    if integer?(x):
        return x * 2
    0

[double(21), double("no"), integer?([1])]
@value [42, 0, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.string?
title: -
summary: Whether a value is a String; inside `if string?(x):` the compiler knows x is a String.
index: stdlib
keywords: is string; is text; type test; kind test; check type
related: concept.types stdlib.integer? concept.strings

== NOTES
A type test like integer?: exact, never failing, and the branch it guards
knows the argument is a String. A UnicodeChar is not a String, so
string?('a') is false.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
[string?("text"), string?('t'), string?(1)]
@value [true, false, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.list?
title: -
summary: Whether a value is a List; inside `if list?(x):` the compiler knows x is a List.
index: stdlib
keywords: is list; is array; type test; kind test; check type
related: concept.types stdlib.integer? concept.lists

== NOTES
A type test like integer?: exact, never failing, and the branch it guards
knows the argument is a List. A MutableArray or MutableVector is not a
List (use mutarray? for an array; there is no test for a vector).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
[list?([]), list?([1, 2]), list?("[1]")]
@value [true, true, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.mutarray?
title: -
summary: Whether a value is a MutableArray; inside `if mutarray?(x):` the compiler knows x is one.
index: stdlib
keywords: is array; is mutable array; type test; kind test
related: concept.mutable-array stdlib.module.std::mutable_array stdlib.integer?

== NOTES
A type test like integer?: exact, never failing, allocating nothing. An
argument already typed MutableArray[T] keeps that element type in the
branch. A List is not a MutableArray, and there is no corresponding test
for MutableVector.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::from_list([1, 2])
[mutarray?(a), mutarray?([1, 2])]
@value [true, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.ok?
title: -
summary: Whether a value is an ok Result; a source program has no way to build a Result, so for its values it is always false.
index: stdlib
keywords: result type; ok result; type test
related: stdlib.error? concept.errors

== NOTES
A type test of the Result kind's ok tag. Result values (ok(v) and
error(v)) exist in the runtime's value model and are built by core IR's
`ok` and `error-value` forms, but the surface language has no syntax and
no standard operation that builds one: `ok(1)` in a source program is an
unbound name. So in a source program ok? and error? answer false for every
value. Failure in Botlish source is a declared error (fail, on, errors),
not a Result (concept.errors).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
[ok?(1), ok?("ok")]
@value [false, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.error?
title: -
summary: Whether a value is an error Result; a source program has no way to build a Result, so for its values it is always false.
index: stdlib
keywords: result type; error result; type test
related: stdlib.ok? concept.errors

== NOTES
The type test of the Result kind's error tag; see stdlib.ok?. It does not
test for a declared error: a declared error is not a value at all, it is
a completion that `on NAME:` handles (concept.errors).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
[error?(1), error?([])]
@value [false, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.hash
title: -
summary: The structural hash of any value that has equality, an Int in 0..2^61-1: equal values (==) always hash equal.
index: stdlib
keywords: hash code; hashing; hash table; bucket; fingerprint
related: stdlib.== stdlib.mod concept.values concept.hashable

== NOTES
hash(v) is a language primitive, exactly as general as ==: Ints, Strings,
Bools, unit, characters, Lists, structs, enum cases, ImmutableSets and
byte storage all hash, and two values that are == hash to the same Int.
The result is nonnegative and below 2^61, so hash(key).mod(capacity) is a
valid table slot. It is also the one operation of the built-in trait
Hashable (concept.hashable): `x.hash()` on a Hashable value.

The value is deterministic: the same on every backend, in every run and
every process, with no random seed. It is not promised across compiler
versions, and it is not cryptographic: never persist it or use it for
security, and do not rely on it against keys an adversary chooses (the
standard library's hash structures use a separate, collision-hardened hash
for that, which no program sees).

A function, MutableArray or MutableVector has no hash. Where the argument's
type shows it is not Hashable (a function, a struct with a function field)
the call is rejected at compile time (TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED); an argument of
unknown type (an untyped parameter) is checked when the call runs (the
EQUALITY error); an affine value is rejected at compile time
(AFFINE-EQUALITY-UNSUPPORTED). The hash of a Hashable value never fails.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
key = "apple"
[
    hash(key) == hash("apple"),
    hash([1, 2]) == hash([1, 2]),
    hash(key).mod(16) < 16
]
@value [true, true, true]
@end

@topic stdlib.argv
title: -
summary: The process's argument vector as a List of Strings, argument zero included; fails with InvalidArgumentEncoding if an argument is not UTF-8.
index: stdlib
keywords: command line arguments; program arguments; args; parameters; process arguments
related: concept.argv task.read-arguments concept.errors

== NOTES
argv() is an ordinary call of a language primitive (no import): it returns
a List[str] whose element 0 is the invocation name the launching process
supplied (not necessarily a path to the executable), followed by the
arguments. Every call of one run sees the same arguments.

Each argument is validated as strict UTF-8 when argv() is called; one
invalid argument fails the whole call with the builtin declared error
InvalidArgumentEncoding (no partial List, no lossy decoding). So a call
must handle it (`on InvalidArgumentEncoding:`), admit it in its
function's errors clause, or let it reach top level, which is rejected as
UNHANDLED-ERROR. The error carries no payload.

Running under main.tcl, -argv TEXT appends one argument (the first one is
argument zero), -argv-hex HEX one given as bytes, and -argv-none gives an
empty vector; with none of them, argv() is ["botlish-runner"]. A native
executable reads its real process arguments. It works on every backend.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

args = argv():
    on InvalidArgumentEncoding:
        []
args.length() >= 1
@value true
@caption Handle the encoding error
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::list
title: -
summary: Immutable List operations: length, indexed access (at, get), append, and the search helpers any?, all?, none? and find.
index: stdlib
keywords: list functions; array functions; sequence operations
related: concept.lists concept.list-length task.list-element capability.map-filter

== NOTES Four intrinsics (std::list::length, std::list::at,
std::list::append and the length validator std::list::with_length) and the
ordinary Botlish functions of stdlib/list.bot built on them. `import
std::list` makes both callable, qualified (std::list::at(xs, 0)) or as
methods (xs.at(0)). A List is immutable: append returns a new List and
nothing changes one in place (a MutableVector is the growable mutable
alternative).

There is deliberately no map, filter, reduce, sum, reverse, sort, slice,
concatenation of two Lists or index-of: a collecting loop
(`loop x in xs: f(x)`, with `continue` to skip) is the transformation
(capability.map-filter), and std::list::any?, all?, none? and find cover
search.

@topic stdlib.std::list::length
title: -
summary: The number of elements of a List, written xs.length() after `import std::list`.
index: stdlib
keywords: size; count; number of elements; how many; len
related: concept.lists stdlib.std::list::at stdlib.std::str::length proof.list-bounds

== NOTES
Never fails and does not look at the elements. Its result is what the
index proof relates indices to: a loop `loop i from 0 to xs.length():`
or a guard on `xs.length()` makes xs.at(i) provably in range
(proof.list-bounds).

The method spelling resolves among the visible functions named length: with
only `import std::str`, `xs.length()` means std::str::length and fails at
run time on a List, so import std::list wherever Lists are measured.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

[[1, 2, 3].length(), [].length(), [[1, 2]].length()]
@value [3, 0, 1]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::list::at
title: -
summary: The element of a List at index i (from 0), written xs.at(i); any other index fails with the declared error IndexNotFound.
index: stdlib
keywords: index; element at; get element; subscript; nth element; lookup
related: proof.list-bounds task.list-element stdlib.std::list::get rule.unhandled-errors rule.known-error concept.lists

== NOTES
Valid indices are 0 to xs.length() - 1. Any other Int -- past the end,
negative (there is no negative indexing: -1 is not "the last"), or huge
-- completes with the builtin declared error IndexNotFound: an ordinary
error a handler can catch, never a crash or a default. A non-List or
non-Int argument is the TYPE error instead.

Because IndexNotFound is declared, each call must handle it
(`on IndexNotFound:`), admit it in its function's errors clause, or be
proven unable to raise it; the compiler proves the common safe forms -- a
counted loop over the List's own length, a dominating length guard, a
literal List with a constant index (proof.list-bounds). A call proven to
always fail (a literal index past a literal List) is rejected as
KNOWN-ERROR even with a handler. When a fallback value is what you want,
std::list::get(xs, i, default) is at with the error handled.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn third(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    v = xs.at(2):
        on IndexNotFound:
            -1
    v

[[10, 20, 30].at(1), third([1, 2, 3]), third([1])]
@value [20, 3, -1]
@caption A proven index and a handled one
@end

@topic stdlib.std::list::append
title: -
summary: A new List of the elements of xs followed by one value, written xs.append(v); xs itself is unchanged.
index: stdlib
keywords: add element; push; append item; insert at end; extend
related: concept.lists stdlib.list stdlib.module.std::mutable_vector stdlib.std::str::append_char

== NOTES
Lists are immutable values, so append builds a new List one element
longer and every other holder of xs still sees the old one. It appends
exactly one value: appending a List adds it as a single, nested element.
There is no operation that joins two Lists; build the combined List with
a collecting loop, or use a MutableVector (std::mutable_vector::push) when
many elements are added one by one.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

xs = [1, 2]
ys = xs.append(3)
[xs, ys, xs.append([4])]
@value [[1, 2], [1, 2, 3], [1, 2, [4]]]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::list::with_length
title: -
summary: std::list::with_length[List[T]{length: N}](xs): xs itself, now known to have exactly N elements, when it has; otherwise the declared error LengthMismatch, after `import std::list`.
index: stdlib
keywords: check length; validate length; exact length; assert length; fixed length; LengthMismatch
related: concept.list-length concept.scalar-refinements stdlib.std::list::length stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::round rule.unhandled-errors

== NOTES
The explicit way to mint a List length refinement (concept.list-length)
from a List whose length is not known statically. It compares the length
once and returns its argument unchanged -- no copy, no new List -- with the
type of its imprint; any other length completes with the builtin declared
error LengthMismatch. The imprint is explicit and must be a refined List
type (std::list::with_length[List[int]](xs) is a TYPE error, and without [T]
it is IMPRINT); the argument must already be a List of the imprint's
element type, since the check proves a length, never elements. Where a
guard reads better, `if xs.length() == N:` refines xs in its branch
without a call. Runs on every backend.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn triple(xs: List[int]) -> List[int]{length: 3} errors LengthMismatch:
    std::list::with_length[List[int]{length: 3}](xs)

fn middle(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    t = xs.triple():
        on LengthMismatch:
            return -1
    t.at(1)

[middle([4, 5, 6]), middle([4, 5])]
@value [5, -1]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::list::get
title: -
summary: The element at index i, or the given default when i designates no element: std::list::at with IndexNotFound handled; written xs.get(i, default).
index: stdlib
keywords: index with default; safe index; element or default; lookup with fallback
related: stdlib.std::list::at task.list-element capability.null

== NOTES
An ordinary function of stdlib/list.bot: std::list::at(xs, index) with
exactly its IndexNotFound handled by returning default. So a call needs no
handler. The default is an ordinary argument, evaluated before the call
whether or not it is used (there is no lazy default), and the result is
never an optional or null value. Other failures of std::list::at -- a
non-List or non-Int argument -- still fail. The result type joins the
element type and the default's: std::list::get([1, 2], 5, 0) is an Int.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

xs = [10, 20, 30]
[xs.get(1, 0), xs.get(7, 0), xs.get(-1, 0)]
@value [20, 0, 0]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::list::any?
title: -
summary: Whether some element of a List satisfies the predicate, left to right, stopping at the first that does; false for the empty List.
index: stdlib
keywords: exists; some; contains matching; any element; search
related: stdlib.std::list::all? stdlib.std::list::none? stdlib.std::list::find capability.map-filter capability.lambdas

== NOTES
The predicate is a function value of one argument returning a Bool --
a named function, or a nested one declared just before the call, since
there are no anonymous function expressions (capability.lambdas). It is
called at most once per element, in order, and not at all after the
first true.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn even?(n: int) -> bool:
    n.mod(2) == 0

[[1, 3, 4].any?(even?), [1, 3].any?(even?), [].any?(even?)]
@value [true, false, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::list::all?
title: -
summary: Whether every element of a List satisfies the predicate, left to right, stopping at the first that does not; true for the empty List.
index: stdlib
keywords: every; for all; all elements; check each
related: stdlib.std::list::any? stdlib.std::list::none? capability.map-filter

== NOTES
The predicate is a function value of one argument returning a Bool (see
std::list::any?); it is called in order and not after the first false.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn positive?(n: int) -> bool:
    n > 0

[
    [1, 2].all?(positive?),
    [1, -2].all?(positive?),
    [].all?(positive?)
]
@value [true, false, true]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::list::none?
title: -
summary: Whether no element of a List satisfies the predicate, left to right, stopping at the first that does; true for the empty List.
index: stdlib
keywords: no element; nothing matches; not any
related: stdlib.std::list::any? stdlib.std::list::all? capability.map-filter

== NOTES
The predicate is a function value of one argument returning a Bool (see
std::list::any?); it is called in order and not after the first true. It
gives the same answers as `not xs.any?(predicate)`.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn negative?(n: int) -> bool:
    n < 0

[[1, 2].none?(negative?), [1, -2].none?(negative?)]
@value [true, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::list::find
title: -
summary: The first element of a List that satisfies the predicate, written xs.find(p); no match fails with the declared error std::list::NotFound.
index: stdlib
keywords: search; first match; find element; locate; filter first
related: stdlib.std::list::any? stdlib.std::list::NotFound capability.null concept.errors

== NOTES
Searches left to right and stops at the first match. When nothing matches
(an empty List included) it fails with std::list::NotFound -- never a null,
a sentinel or an optional value -- so a call handles it (`on
std::list::NotFound:`, the error's module-qualified name), admits it in its
function's errors clause, or is rejected as UNHANDLED-ERROR. There is no
index-of: to find a position, loop over the indices.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn even?(n: int) -> bool:
    n.mod(2) == 0

fn first_even(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    v = xs.find(even?):
        on std::list::NotFound:
            -1
    v

[first_even([3, 8, 6]), first_even([1, 5])]
@value [8, -1]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::list::NotFound
title: -
summary: The declared error std::list::find fails with when no element satisfies its predicate; handled as `on std::list::NotFound:`.
index: stdlib
keywords: not found; no match; search failed
related: stdlib.std::list::find concept.errors

== NOTES
A payload-free declared error of module list. Outside stdlib/list.bot it is
spelled std::list::NotFound, in `on` handlers and errors clauses alike (an
error's identity is module-qualified). It is not IndexNotFound, the
builtin error of std::list::at.

@topic stdlib.module.std::str
title: -
summary: String operations: length, slices, concatenation, appending a character, character access, UTF-8 bytes and lowercase.
index: stdlib
keywords: string functions; text functions; text operations
related: concept.strings task.string-concatenation capability.string-formatting task.number-text

== NOTES
Every member is an intrinsic; there is no stdlib/str.bot. `import std::str`
makes them callable qualified (std::str::concat(a, b)) or as methods
(a.concat(b)). Strings are immutable sequences of Unicode scalar values,
and every length and index counts those -- never bytes, never grapheme
clusters.

What is not here: no uppercase, trim, split, join of a List, search,
replace, comparison or ordering, and no formatting
(capability.string-formatting); a number's text and its parsing are
std::int's (task.number-text). Equality is == (stdlib.==).

@topic stdlib.std::str::length
title: -
summary: The number of characters (Unicode scalar values) of a String, written s.length() after `import std::str`; not bytes.
index: stdlib
keywords: string length; size; count characters; number of characters; len
related: concept.strings stdlib.std::str::char_at stdlib.std::str::substring stdlib.std::list::length proof.slices

== NOTES
Counts Unicode scalar values: "naïve" has 5 and "😀" 1, whatever their UTF-8
size (std::list::length(s.encode_utf8()) is the byte count). A character
written as a letter plus a combining accent counts as two. Never fails. Its
result bounds the indices of std::str::char_at and std::str::substring, and
the compiler relates guards on it to those calls (proof.slices).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

word = "naïve"
[word.length(), "".length(), "a😀b".length()]
@value [5, 0, 3]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::str::substring
title: -
summary: The characters of a String from index start up to but not including end, written s.substring(start, end); an invalid slice fails with LowerUnderrun or UpperOverrun.
index: stdlib
keywords: slice; substring; part of string; cut; prefix; suffix
related: proof.slices concept.strings stdlib.std::str::char_at stdlib.std::str::length rule.unhandled-errors

== NOTES
A half-open slice start..end over character indices, valid exactly when
0 <= start <= end <= s.length(). Start is checked first, against
0..length, then end against start..length: a bound below its interval
fails with the builtin declared error LowerUnderrun, one above it with
UpperOverrun. So an inverted slice (end < start) is LowerUnderrun, and
"abc".substring(4, 1) is UpperOverrun for its start.

Like every declared error they must be handled, admitted or proven
impossible. The compiler proves slices from String lengths it knows: a
literal, a std::str::concat of known lengths, and guards such as
`if s.length() > 0:` (proof.slices). The result is a new plain String
(a refinement of s is not carried over).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

fn initial(s: str) -> str:
    if s.length() > 0:
        return s.substring(0, 1)
    ""

["hello".substring(1, 3), initial("Botlish"), initial("")]
@value ["el", "B", ""]
@caption Proven slices need no handler
@end

@example accept
import std::str

fn cut(s: str, a: int, b: int) -> str:
    t = s.substring(a, b):
        on LowerUnderrun:
            "below"
        on UpperOverrun:
            "above"
    t

["abc".cut(2, 1), "abc".cut(1, 9), "abc".cut(3, 3)]
@value ["below", "above", ""]
@caption Unproven slices handle both errors
@end

@topic stdlib.std::str::lowercase
title: -
summary: The String with every character mapped to lowercase by Unicode's simple one-to-one mapping, written s.lowercase().
index: stdlib
keywords: lower case; to lowercase; case conversion; downcase; case insensitive
related: concept.strings stdlib.module.std::str

== NOTES
Uses the Unicode 16 simple (one-to-one) lowercase mapping on every
backend: each character maps to exactly one character, so the length
never changes. The full SpecialCasing rules are not applied ("İ" becomes
"i", without a combining dot; a final sigma is "σ", not "ς"), and there is
no locale. Characters without a lowercase form are unchanged. There is no
uppercase or case-folding operation; to compare case-insensitively,
compare the lowercased Strings.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

["Hello, World".lowercase(), "ÀB-Ⱥ".lowercase(), "ΑΣ".lowercase()]
@value ["hello, world", "àb-ⱥ", "ασ"]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::str::concat
title: -
summary: The String A followed by the String B, written a.concat(b) after `import std::str`: a new String; neither argument changes.
index: stdlib
keywords: join strings; append text; combine strings; concatenate; build text
related: task.string-concatenation concept.strings stdlib.std::str::append_char capability.string-formatting

== NOTES
The way to join text: + is for Ints only ("a" + "b" is the TYPE error).
Both arguments must be Strings; to add a single character use
std::str::append_char, and to put a number into text convert it first
(std::int::to_string, task.number-text). The length of the result is the
sum of the lengths, which the slice proof knows (proof.slices). For many
pieces, chain the calls: a.concat(b).concat(c).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

name = "world"
["Hello, ".concat(name), "ab".concat("").concat("cd")]
@value ["Hello, world", "abcd"]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::str::append_char
title: -
summary: The String followed by one UnicodeChar, written s.append_char(c): how a character becomes text, without a one-character String.
index: stdlib
keywords: add character; append char; push character; character to string; build text
related: concept.strings stdlib.std::str::concat stdlib.std::str::char_at principle.characters-not-strings warning.one-char-string-literal

== NOTES
Returns a new String one character longer (std::str::length(s) + 1); s is
unchanged. It is to a String what std::list::append is to a List. The second
argument is a UnicodeChar -- a character literal such as '!' or '\n', or
a std::str::char_at result -- never a String, so a character the code holds
never has to become a one-character String first (the
ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL warning recommends exactly this). Total: every
UnicodeChar, astral ones included, can be appended.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

line = "done".append_char('!').append_char('\n')
[line, line.length()]
@value ["done!\n", 6]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::str::encode_utf8
title: -
summary: The UTF-8 encoding of a String as a List of byte values (each a std::byte::Byte, 0..255), written s.encode_utf8().
index: stdlib
keywords: utf-8; utf8 bytes; encode; string to bytes; byte count
related: concept.strings stdlib.module.std::byte stdlib.std::abi::bytes::from_list stdlib.std::str::char_at

== NOTES
The general byte-level view of a String: one Int per byte, in order. Its
static type is List[std::byte::Byte], so it feeds std::abi::bytes::from_list
(the bytes a write(2) or a C call takes) and ascii:: classification
directly. Its length is the String's size in bytes, not its character count.
There is no decoding operation back from bytes to a String. To classify
characters, prefer std::str::char_at, which reads a character without
building a List.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list
import std::str

["Aé".encode_utf8(), "€uro".encode_utf8().length(), "€uro".length()]
@value [[65, 195, 169], 6, 4]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::str::char_at
title: -
summary: The character (UnicodeChar) at character index i of a String, written s.char_at(i); any other index fails with IndexNotFound.
index: stdlib
keywords: character at; char at; index string; nth character; get character
related: proof.list-bounds concept.strings stdlib.std::char::scalar_value stdlib.std::str::append_char stdlib.std::str::length

== NOTES
Valid indices are 0 to s.length() - 1, in characters (the index space of
length and substring); any other Int fails with the builtin declared error
IndexNotFound, exactly like std::list::at, and the same proofs apply (a
counted loop to s.length(), a dominating guard, a literal String). It never
allocates: compare its result with character literals (c == ',') or measure
it with std::char::scalar_value to scan a String without making a
one-character String per position. There is no std::str::at.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::char
import std::list
import std::str

fn digit_count(s: str) -> int:
    hits = loop i from 0 to s.length():
        n = s.char_at(i).scalar_value()
        if n < 48 or n > 57:
            continue
        n
    hits.length()

["héllo".char_at(1), digit_count("a1b22")]
@value ['é', 3]
@caption A counted loop needs no handler
@end

@topic stdlib.std::str::is_tcl_alpha
title: -
summary: Whether a one-character String is a letter (Unicode general category L*), exactly as Tcl 9's [:alpha:] decides; a temporary compatibility primitive.
index: stdlib
keywords: is letter; alphabetic; character class; unicode letter
related: stdlib.std::str::is_tcl_alnum stdlib.std::char::is_tcl_alpha stdlib.module.std::ascii stdlib.std::web::emailish?

== NOTES
Exists so that stdlib/web.bot's emailish? can reproduce Tcl's character
classes; "tcl" in the name says it is not Botlish's eventual
character-classification API, so new code should not build on it. True
for general categories Lu, Ll, Lt, Lm and Lo -- Unicode-aware, not
ASCII-only. The argument must be a String of exactly one character;
anything else (the empty String included) is the run-time RANGE error.
It takes a String, not a UnicodeChar, so pass a one-character slice; for a
character (std::str::char_at's result) std::char::is_tcl_alpha decides the
same class without a slice. For byte values, std::ascii::is_alphabetic is
the structural ASCII test.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

text = "é7"
[
    std::str::is_tcl_alpha(text.substring(0, 1)),
    std::str::is_tcl_alpha(text.substring(1, 2))
]
@value [true, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::str::is_tcl_alnum
title: -
summary: Whether a one-character String is a letter or a decimal digit (categories L* and Nd), exactly as Tcl 9's [:alnum:] decides; a temporary compatibility primitive.
index: stdlib
keywords: is alphanumeric; letter or digit; character class
related: stdlib.std::str::is_tcl_alpha stdlib.std::char::is_tcl_alnum stdlib.std::ascii::is_alphanumeric

== NOTES
std::str::is_tcl_alpha plus general category Nd (decimal numbers, in any
script); other numerals (Roman numerals, superscripts) are not included.
Same contract as std::str::is_tcl_alpha: exactly one character or the
run-time RANGE error, and a temporary compatibility primitive rather than a
general API. For a character, std::char::is_tcl_alnum decides the same
class.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

text = "7-"
[
    std::str::is_tcl_alnum(text.substring(0, 1)),
    std::str::is_tcl_alnum(text.substring(1, 2))
]
@value [true, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::char
title: -
summary: The UnicodeChar operations: std::char::scalar_value, a character's Unicode scalar value as an Int, and the character classes std::char::is_tcl_alpha and std::char::is_tcl_alnum.
index: stdlib
keywords: character functions; char functions; unicode character; character class
related: concept.strings stdlib.std::str::char_at stdlib.std::str::append_char stdlib.std::char::is_tcl_alpha stdlib.std::char::is_tcl_alnum

== NOTES
`char` has no module file: its members are intrinsics. A UnicodeChar is
written as a character literal ('a', '\n', '😀') and read from a String with
std::str::char_at; it becomes text with std::str::append_char. There is no
conversion from an Int to a UnicodeChar. A character is classified with
std::char::is_tcl_alpha and std::char::is_tcl_alnum (Tcl 9's [:alpha:] and
[:alnum:], the classes the temporary std::str forms decide for a
one-character String), compared with character literals (c == '_'), or
measured with std::char::scalar_value (with the byte-level ascii::
predicates for an ASCII scalar).

@topic stdlib.std::char::scalar_value
title: -
summary: The Unicode scalar value of a character as an Int (0..0x10FFFF, never a surrogate), written c.scalar_value() after `import std::char`.
index: stdlib
keywords: code point; character code; ord; unicode number; char to int
related: stdlib.module.std::char stdlib.std::str::char_at concept.strings stdlib.std::byte::set

== NOTES
Total: every UnicodeChar has exactly one scalar value, and the result is
known to lie in 0..1114111, which the range analysis uses. It is the number
of the Unicode scalar, never a byte of an encoding or a UTF-16 unit: 'é' is
233 and '😀' 128512. Its parameter is a UnicodeChar, checked when the call
runs (std::char::scalar_value(65) is the TYPE error). There is no inverse:
no operation makes a character from an Int.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::char

['A'.scalar_value(), 'é'.scalar_value(), '😀'.scalar_value()]
@value [65, 233, 128512]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::char::is_tcl_alpha
title: -
summary: Whether a character (UnicodeChar) is a letter (Unicode general category L*), exactly as Tcl 9's [:alpha:] decides: std::str::is_tcl_alpha's class, for a character instead of a one-character String.
index: stdlib
keywords: is letter; alphabetic; character class; unicode letter; classify character
related: stdlib.module.std::char stdlib.std::char::is_tcl_alnum stdlib.std::str::is_tcl_alpha stdlib.std::str::char_at stdlib.std::web::emailish?

== NOTES
True for general categories Lu, Ll, Lt, Lm and Lo -- Unicode-aware, not
ASCII-only -- and for no other character: not a decimal digit, a Roman
numeral (Nl), a combining mark or punctuation. It decides what
std::str::is_tcl_alpha decides for the one-character String of the same
character, but it is total -- every UnicodeChar is one character, so there
is no RANGE case -- and it never makes a String: read the characters of a
text with std::str::char_at and classify them as they are
(UNICODECHAR-CLASSIFIERS.md). Like the std::str form, it reproduces Tcl's
class rather than being Botlish's eventual character-classification API.
Its parameter is a UnicodeChar: a one-character String is the TYPE error,
and a method-style call x.is_tcl_alpha() with both std::char and std::str
imported needs x's type to choose (with an untyped x it is
AMBIGUOUS-METHOD-CALL: call the one you mean).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::char
import std::str

text = "é7例"
loop i from 0 to text.length():
    std::char::is_tcl_alpha(text.char_at(i))
@value [true, false, true]
@caption A character read from a String, classified without a slice
@end

@topic stdlib.std::char::is_tcl_alnum
title: -
summary: Whether a character (UnicodeChar) is a letter or a decimal digit (categories L* and Nd), exactly as Tcl 9's [:alnum:] decides: std::str::is_tcl_alnum's class, for a character.
index: stdlib
keywords: is alphanumeric; letter or digit; character class; classify character
related: stdlib.module.std::char stdlib.std::char::is_tcl_alpha stdlib.std::str::is_tcl_alnum stdlib.std::ascii::is_alphanumeric

== NOTES
std::char::is_tcl_alpha plus general category Nd (decimal numbers, in any
script: ARABIC-INDIC DIGIT ZERO is alnum and not alpha); other numerals
(Roman numerals, circled digits, fractions, superscripts) are not included.
Total and allocation-free, like std::char::is_tcl_alpha; it decides what
std::str::is_tcl_alnum decides for the one-character String of the same
character.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::char

['7'.is_tcl_alnum(), '٠'.is_tcl_alnum(), 'Ⅻ'.is_tcl_alnum(), '_'.is_tcl_alnum()]
@value [true, true, false, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::decimal_rounded
title: -
summary: The explicit quantization from Float64 to DecimalRounded, std::decimal_rounded::round[T](x), and a value's guaranteed decimal as text, std::decimal_rounded::to_string(x).
index: stdlib
keywords: decimal functions; decimal conversion; float to decimal; quantize; round to places; decimal to string; format decimal
related: concept.decimal-rounded stdlib.std::float64::from_decimal_rounded stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::to_string

== NOTES
`decimal_rounded` has no module file: its members are intrinsics, reached
with `import std::decimal_rounded`. With
std::float64::from_decimal_rounded, round is one of the two conversions
between DecimalRounded and Float64: nothing converts implicitly, a Float64
never becomes a DecimalRounded value by being relabeled, and the operators
on DecimalRounded values are the language's own (concept.decimal-rounded).
to_string is the value's text at the precision its type guarantees.

@topic stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::round
title: -
summary: std::decimal_rounded::round[T](x): the value of the DecimalRounded type T whose decimal is the Float64 x rounded half to even to T's places, after `import std::decimal_rounded`.
index: stdlib
keywords: round to places; quantize; float to decimal; make decimal; banker's rounding
related: concept.decimal-rounded stdlib.std::float64::from_decimal_rounded stdlib.module.std::decimal_rounded error.decimal-guarantee-lost

== NOTES
The one way to mint a guarantee from a value computed at run time: it
does not claim x already is a decimal, it rounds it. T is imprinted
explicitly and must be a refined DecimalRounded type (decimal_rounded::
round[Float64](x) is a TYPE error, and without [T] it is IMPRINT). The
rounding reads x's exact binary value: the Float64 1.005 is
1.00499999999999989..., so it rounds to 1.00 at two places. The result is
represented to satisfy every place up to N; when no representation does
-- a decimal finer than binary64 holds at its magnitude, such as
4873863637.854281 at nine places -- the call fails with the declared
error UnrepresentableDecimal. Runs on every backend.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::decimal_rounded

fn cents(x: Float64) -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2} errors UnrepresentableDecimal:
    std::decimal_rounded::round[DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}](x)

fn safe(x: Float64) -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}:
    x.cents():
        on UnrepresentableDecimal:
            0.00

[safe(0.1 + 0.2), safe(1.005), safe(-0.125)]
@value [0.30, 1.00, -0.12]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::to_string
title: -
summary: The guaranteed decimal of the DecimalRounded value x as a String: round(x, N) with exactly N fraction digits, N its type's minDecimalsPreserved, after `import std::decimal_rounded`.
index: stdlib
keywords: decimal to string; format decimal; show decimal; decimal text; print money; to_string
related: concept.decimal-rounded stdlib.module.std::decimal_rounded stdlib.std::int::to_string stdlib.std::float64::from_decimal_rounded

== NOTES
The text a DecimalRounded value guarantees, never the digits of the
binary64 behind it: x rounded half to even to the N places of its static
type (DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: N}), written with exactly N
digits after the point -- trailing zeros kept, no point at N = 0 -- and a
"-" only before a nonzero result (DecimalRounded has no negative zero:
-0.04 at one place is "0.0"). It is the same text a program's printed
result shows. The precision is the argument's static type's: the compiler
writes it into the call, so a value carries none, and a value weakened to
fewer places shows those (a DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 3} value
passed as one with 1 shows one place). maxDecimalsPreserved changes
nothing. An argument of another type (a Float64, an Int) is a TYPE error;
one whose type is erased (an untyped parameter) has no precision to show
and is refused when the call runs. Never fails; runs on every backend;
also written x.to_string().

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::decimal_rounded

fn cents(x: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}) -> str:
    x.to_string()

fn tenths(x: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 1}) -> str:
    std::decimal_rounded::to_string(x)

[cents(0.295), cents(1.245), cents(25.0), cents(-0.004), tenths(6.0)]
@value ["0.30", "1.24", "25.00", "0.00", "6.0"]
@caption Exactly the guaranteed places: 0.295 shows 0.30 (not its binary64's digits), a half rounds to even, zeros stay
@end

@example reject
import std::decimal_rounded

fn t(x: Float64) -> str:
    std::decimal_rounded::to_string(x)

t(1.5)
@caption A Float64 is no DecimalRounded value: quantize it first with std::decimal_rounded::round[T]
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 4:5
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::float64
title: -
summary: Float64's explicit conversions (std::float64::from_int, std::float64::truncate, std::float64::round, std::float64::from_decimal_rounded) and its bounds (std::float64::min, max, abs, clamp).
index: stdlib
keywords: float functions; float conversion; int to float; float to int; decimal to float; float min; float max; float abs; clamp; round
related: concept.floats stdlib./ concept.decimal-rounded stdlib.std::float64::round

== NOTES The conversions are intrinsics and the bounds (min, max, abs,
clamp) the ordinary Botlish of stdlib/float64.bot, all reached with `import
std::float64`. The conversions are the only ones between Float64 and the
other number types: Botlish never converts implicitly, so an expression
mixing an Int and a Float64 is rejected until one side is converted (and so
is one mixing a Float64 and a DecimalRounded value). The bounds never fail:
they compare and at most negate, so no NonFiniteFloat can arise. There is no
rounding mode, no Float64-to-String conversion beyond a program's printed
result, no math library beyond these, and no bit-level access to a Float64.

@topic stdlib.std::float64::from_decimal_rounded
title: -
summary: The Float64 nearest round(x, N), N the places the DecimalRounded x's type guarantees, after `import std::float64`: the visible decay of a guarantee; it never fails.
index: stdlib
keywords: decimal to float; decay; forget precision; convert decimal
related: concept.decimal-rounded stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::round stdlib.module.std::float64 error.decimal-guarantee-lost

== NOTES
Decays a DecimalRounded value to a Float64 explicitly: the guarantee is
forgotten, and the Float64 is the one nearest the guaranteed decimal --
never the value's hidden representation digits, so every backend and
every representation give the same Float64. The decimal is round(x, N)
with N x's static minDecimalsPreserved (a value of
DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2} representing 0.295 gives 0.3).
It is how a program computes with DecimalRounded values that are not
constants (error.decimal-guarantee-lost).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::float64

fn cents() -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}:
    0.295

[std::float64::from_decimal_rounded(cents()), cents().from_decimal_rounded()]
@value [0.3, 0.3]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::float64::from_int
title: -
summary: The Float64 nearest the Int N (ties to even), after `import std::float64`; an Int beyond the largest Float64 fails with NonFiniteFloat.
index: stdlib
keywords: int to float; convert int; float from int; to float
related: concept.floats stdlib.std::float64::truncate stdlib.module.std::float64

== NOTES
Rounds to the nearest Float64, ties to even, from the exact Int however
large: 2^53 + 1 becomes 9007199254740992.0. An Int whose magnitude is
beyond the largest finite Float64 (about 1.8e308) has no finite nearest
value and fails with NonFiniteFloat, decided at compile time when the Int
is known.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::float64

[std::float64::from_int(3), std::float64::from_int(-12), std::float64::from_int(9007199254740993)]
@value [3.0, -12.0, 9007199254740992.0]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::float64::truncate
title: -
summary: The Int part of the Float64 X, rounding toward zero, after `import std::float64`: exact at any magnitude, and it never fails.
index: stdlib
keywords: float to int; convert float; truncate; integer part; round toward zero
related: concept.floats stdlib.std::float64::from_int stdlib.module.std::float64

== NOTES
Drops the fraction: 2.75 becomes 2 and -2.75 becomes -2. Every Float64 is
finite, so every one has an integer part, and the Int holds it exactly
however large it is (1.0e20 becomes 100000000000000000000).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::float64

big = 1.0e20
[std::float64::truncate(2.75), std::float64::truncate(-2.75), big.truncate()]
@value [2, -2, 100000000000000000000]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::float64::round
title: -
summary: The Int nearest the Float64 X, a half to the even Int, after `import std::float64`: exact at any magnitude, and it never fails.
index: stdlib
keywords: float to int; convert float; round; nearest integer; round half to even; banker's rounding
related: concept.floats stdlib.std::float64::truncate stdlib.module.std::float64 concept.decimal-rounded

== NOTES
Rounds to the nearest Int; a value exactly halfway between two Ints goes
to the even one (0.5 and -0.5 to 0, 1.5 and 2.5 to 2, -2.5 to -2) -- the
rule Float64 arithmetic and DecimalRounded already round by, so there is
one rounding rule in the language. It reads the Float64's exact binary
value, not its shortest decimal text: 0.49999999999999994 (one ulp below
0.5) rounds to 0 and 2.5000000000000004 to 3. Every Float64 is finite, so
every one rounds to some Int, held exactly however large (1.0e20 becomes
100000000000000000000); no overflow error. std::float64::truncate goes
toward zero instead (2.7 to 2, where round gives 3). There is no floor or
ceiling. Also written x.round().

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::float64

halves = [0.5, 1.5, 2.5, -2.5, 2.7, -2.7]
loop x in halves:
    [x.round(), x.truncate()]
@value [[0, 0], [2, 1], [2, 2], [-2, -2], [3, 2], [-3, -2]]
@caption round to nearest, ties to even, beside truncate toward zero
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::int
title: -
summary: An Int's canonical decimal text, both ways: std::int::to_string(n) and std::int::from_string(s), whose malformed text is the declared error InvalidIntegerText.
index: stdlib
keywords: int functions; int to string; string to int; parse int; format int; number to text; text to number; integer text
related: concept.integers stdlib.std::int::to_string stdlib.std::int::from_string task.number-text

== NOTES
`int` has no module file: its members are intrinsics, reached with `import
std::int`. The Int operators themselves (+, mod, bit_and, ...) are root
natives that need no import. The text is the one canonical decimal text of
an Int -- the integer literal's digits, no leading zero, a "-" before a
negative number -- and the two conversions are inverses: from_string
accepts exactly what to_string writes. Base ten only; no locale, no digit
grouping, no "+".

@topic stdlib.std::int::to_string
title: -
summary: std::int::to_string(n): the canonical decimal text of the Int n, exact at any size, after `import std::int`.
index: stdlib
keywords: int to string; format int; number to text; show int; integer text; to_string; itoa
related: stdlib.module.std::int stdlib.std::int::from_string concept.integers stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::to_string

== NOTES
The decimal digits of n, with no leading zero (0 is "0") and a "-" before
a negative number: the text a program's printed result shows for an Int,
for an Int of any size. Never fails; also written n.to_string(). Nothing
turns an Int into text implicitly: "x = ".concat(n) fails with TYPE
(concat takes two Strings), and is written "x = ".concat(n.to_string()).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::int

loop n in [0, 7, -7, 123456, -123456789012345678901234567890]:
    n.to_string()
@value ["0", "7", "-7", "123456", "-123456789012345678901234567890"]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::int::from_string
title: -
summary: std::int::from_string(s): the Int whose canonical decimal text is s, else the declared error InvalidIntegerText, after `import std::int`.
index: stdlib
keywords: string to int; parse int; text to number; read int; atoi; parse number; InvalidIntegerText
related: stdlib.module.std::int stdlib.std::int::to_string rule.unhandled-errors

== NOTES
Accepts exactly the texts std::int::to_string writes -- an optional "-"
and decimal digits, no leading zero -- so from_string(to_string(n)) is n
for every Int, of any size. Everything else completes with the builtin
declared error InvalidIntegerText, which the caller handles or declares:
the empty text, "-", "+1", "01", "-01", "-0" (zero has one text), any
space ("1 ", " 1"), a fraction or exponent ("1.0", "1e3"), "1_000", a
digit other than ASCII 0-9, and trailing text ("12x"). It never clamps,
never stops early and never reads zero for a malformed text. It is
`nomethod` (a String is not the receiver of an Int's parse): write
std::int::from_string(s), not s.from_string().

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::int

fn read(s: str) -> int:
    std::int::from_string(s):
        on InvalidIntegerText:
            -1

loop s in ["42", "-7", "98765432109876543210", "01", "+1", "1 ", "12x", ""]:
    read(s)
@value [42, -7, 98765432109876543210, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1]
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::mutable_array
title: -
summary: MutableArray[T]: a fixed-length mutable value with indexed reads and writes (at, set, swap), slice copies and List snapshots (freeze).
index: stdlib
keywords: array; fixed size array; mutable array; buffer; table
related: concept.mutable-array concept.values stdlib.module.std::mutable_vector proof.list-bounds

== NOTES
A MutableArray is a value, not a reference: `b = a` makes an independent
logical copy (copy-on-write underneath), so a mutation through a is
never seen through b. Its length (capacity) is fixed when it is made.
Mutations (set, swap, copy into it) change the array in place, so their
receiver must be a place -- a local, parameter or context parameter, or a
field path from one -- and a temporary receiver is MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER.

Make one with allocate (slots of unit), create (one value repeated),
from_list or generate (a factory per slot). Read with at or
std::mutable_array::get, write with set or swap, take snapshots with freeze,
and visit the elements with `loop x in a:`. There is no equality or hash
for arrays. An array of affine elements (coroutine handles, ...) is
itself affine: elements move in and out only through set, swap and
consuming loops, and at would copy one out (AFFINE-ELEMENT-COPY-OUT).

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::allocate
title: -
summary: A new MutableArray of n slots, each holding unit, written std::mutable_array::allocate(n): the raw substrate (create(n, x) gives a typed one).
index: stdlib
keywords: new array; allocate array; array of size n; empty slots
related: stdlib.std::mutable_array::create stdlib.module.std::mutable_array concept.mutable-array

== NOTES
Every slot holds unit until it is set. The capacity n must be 0..2^62-1; a
negative or larger n is the run-time RANGE error. The compiler knows the
capacity of an array made from a known n, so `a.at(i)` with i proven in
0..n-1 needs no handler. Usually std::mutable_array::create(n, x) is what
you want: its element type is x's, where allocate's starts as unit.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::allocate(3)
[a.capacity(), a.at(0)]
@value [3, unit]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::create
title: -
summary: A new MutableArray of n slots, each holding the value x, written std::mutable_array::create(n, x) (nomethod).
index: stdlib
keywords: new array; filled array; array of size n; initialize array; repeat value
related: stdlib.std::mutable_array::generate stdlib.std::mutable_array::allocate concept.mutable-array concept.affine

== NOTES
x is repeated: every slot holds a logical copy of the one value, so x must
be unrestricted -- an affine x (a coroutine handle, or a struct or List
owning one) would end up with several owners and is rejected as
AFFINE-DUPLICATION-UNSUPPORTED; use std::mutable_array::generate, which
makes one value per slot. n must be 0..2^62-1 (otherwise the run-time RANGE
error). Declared nomethod: its first argument is a length, not a receiver,
so n.create(x) is NOMETHOD-CALL.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::create(3, 0)
a.set(1, 7)
a.freeze(3)
@value [0, 7, 0]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::from_list
title: -
summary: A new MutableArray holding the elements of a List, in order, written std::mutable_array::from_list(xs).
index: stdlib
keywords: list to array; convert list; array from list
related: stdlib.std::mutable_array::freeze stdlib.module.std::mutable_array concept.mutable-array

== NOTES
The capacity is the List's length, known to the compiler when the List's
is. The List moves into the array (for affine elements the array becomes
their owner); for ordinary values the List stays usable, being an
immutable value. The way back is std::mutable_array::freeze.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::from_list([1, 2, 3])
b = a
a.set(0, 100)
[a.freeze(3), b.freeze(3)]
@value [[100, 2, 3], [1, 2, 3]]
@caption b is an independent copy
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::generate
title: -
summary: A new MutableArray of n slots whose slot i holds f(i), f called once per slot in index order; f's errors propagate.
index: stdlib
keywords: build array; array from function; tabulate; initialize with function; factory
related: stdlib.std::mutable_array::create concept.mutable-array concept.affine proof.callable-contracts

== NOTES
f is a function value of one Int argument, a factory: it is called for
0, 1, ..., n - 1, in order, and each result is owned by its slot, so it
is the way to fill an array with affine values (create would duplicate
one). The call can fail with whatever errors f can raise: the first
failing call ends the construction, and for affine elements the ones
already made are released. Handle or admit those errors as for a call of
f. n must be 0..2^62-1.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

fn square(i: int) -> int:
    i * i

g = std::mutable_array::generate(4, square)
g.freeze(4)
@value [0, 1, 4, 9]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::capacity
title: -
summary: The number of slots of a MutableArray, written a.capacity(): fixed when the array is made.
index: stdlib
keywords: array length; size; count; number of slots; len
related: stdlib.std::mutable_array::at proof.list-bounds stdlib.std::list::length

== NOTES
Never fails. It plays the role std::list::length plays for Lists: a counted
loop `loop i from 0 to a.capacity():` proves a.at(i) and a.set(i, x) in
range (proof.list-bounds). An array never grows; MutableVector is the
growable one.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

fn fill(n: int) -> List[int]:
    a = std::mutable_array::create(n, 0)
    loop i from 0 to a.capacity():
        a.set(i, i * 10)
    a.freeze(a.capacity())

fill(4)
@value [0, 10, 20, 30]
@caption Capacity-bounded indices are proven
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::element
title: -
summary: The place of slot i of a MutableArray an installed context owns, written c.member.element(i): observed where it is stored, never a value; IndexNotFound outside 0..capacity-1.
index: stdlib
keywords: element place; observe slot; context-owned array; affine element
related: stdlib.std::mutable_array::at stdlib.std::mutable_array::swap concept.context-owned-state error.affine-context-copy-out stdlib.std::mutable_vector::element

== NOTES
std::mutable_vector::element's rule for arrays: the receiver is a place of
an installed context, the result the slot's place (observed, projected,
never copied out when affine), IndexNotFound when there is no such slot.
To exchange an affine element, swap it.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

opaque context struct Table:
    cells: MutableArray[int]

fn cell(i: int, context t: Table) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    t.cells.element(i)

fn read(i: int) -> int:
    v = cell(i):
        on IndexNotFound:
            -1
    v

with context Table {cells: std::mutable_array::from_list([4, 5])}
[read(1), read(2)]
@caption A slot read where the installed array stores it
@value [5, -1]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::at
title: -
summary: The element of a MutableArray at index i, written a.at(i); any index outside 0..capacity-1 fails with IndexNotFound.
index: stdlib
keywords: array index; element at; read slot; subscript
related: stdlib.std::list::at stdlib.std::mutable_array::set proof.list-bounds rule.unhandled-errors

== NOTES
The contract and the proofs are std::list::at's, against the capacity: a
counted loop to a.capacity(), a dominating guard, or an array of known
capacity with an index of known range needs no handler; otherwise handle or
admit IndexNotFound, or use std::mutable_array::get with a default. For an
array of affine elements, at would make a second owner and is rejected
(AFFINE-ELEMENT-COPY-OUT): swap the element out instead, or reach it
where it is with std::mutable_array::element when an installed context
owns the array.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::from_list([5, 6, 7])
[a.at(0), a.at(2)]
@value [5, 7]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::set
title: -
summary: Stores x in slot i of a MutableArray in place, written a.set(i, x); returns unit, and an index outside 0..capacity-1 fails with IndexNotFound and writes nothing.
index: stdlib
keywords: array write; store; assign slot; update element; put
related: stdlib.std::mutable_array::at stdlib.std::mutable_array::swap proof.list-bounds concept.mutable-array

== NOTES
The receiver must be a place (a named local, parameter or field path):
`std::mutable_array::from_list(xs).set(0, 1)` is MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER,
since the updated array would be lost. The array changes in place; other
logical copies made earlier do not. x must fit the element type. Index
proofs are those of std::mutable_array::at. For affine elements the
displaced element is released.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::create(2, "")
a.set(1, "hi")
a.freeze(2)
@value ["", "hi"]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::swap
title: -
summary: Stores x in slot i of a MutableArray and returns the element it replaced, written a.swap(i, x); a bad index fails with IndexNotFound and changes nothing.
index: stdlib
keywords: exchange; replace element; take out; move out of array
related: stdlib.std::mutable_array::set stdlib.std::mutable_vector::swap concept.affine

== NOTES
Like set, but the old element comes back instead of being dropped: the
way to move an element out of an array of affine values (the replacement
moves in). The receiver must be a place.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::from_list([1, 2, 3])
old = a.swap(0, 9)
[old, a.freeze(3)]
@value [1, [9, 2, 3]]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::copy
title: -
summary: Copies count elements of src from index ss into dst from index ds, written std::mutable_array::copy(dst, ds, src, ss, count); a bad slice fails with LowerUnderrun or UpperOverrun.
index: stdlib
keywords: copy elements; memmove; blit; array copy; move range
related: proof.slices stdlib.std::mutable_array::freeze stdlib.std::str::substring

== NOTES
Memmove semantics: dst and src may be the same array with overlapping
ranges, and the result is as if every source element were read before
any write. Both ranges are half-open slices, checked like
std::str::substring's: the destination ds..ds+count against dst's capacity
first, then the source ss..ss+count; the first invalid one fails
(LowerUnderrun for a bound below its interval, a negative count
included; UpperOverrun above it) and nothing is copied. Returns unit; dst
must be a place. Declared nomethod: the first argument is the
destination, so dst.copy(...) would misread as copying dst.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::from_list([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])
std::mutable_array::copy(a, 1, a, 0, 3)
a.freeze(5)
@value [1, 1, 2, 3, 5]
@caption An overlapping copy within one array
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::freeze
title: -
summary: An immutable List of the first n elements of a MutableArray, written a.freeze(n); n outside 0..capacity fails with LowerUnderrun or UpperOverrun.
index: stdlib
keywords: array to list; snapshot; convert to list; copy out
related: proof.slices stdlib.std::mutable_array::from_list concept.lists

== NOTES
The slice 0..n of the array, copied into a new List: later mutations of
the array do not affect it. A negative n is LowerUnderrun, one above the
capacity UpperOverrun; a.freeze(a.capacity()) or a literal n within a
known capacity is proven. For an array of affine elements, freeze would
copy them out and is rejected (AFFINE-ELEMENT-COPY-OUT).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::from_list([4, 5, 6])
[a.freeze(2), a.freeze(0), a.freeze(a.capacity())]
@value [[4, 5], [], [4, 5, 6]]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_array::get
title: -
summary: The element at index i of a MutableArray, or the given default when i designates no slot: std::mutable_array::at with IndexNotFound handled; written a.get(i, default).
index: stdlib
keywords: index with default; safe index; element or default
related: stdlib.std::mutable_array::at stdlib.std::list::get

== NOTES
An ordinary function of stdlib/mutable_array.bot, the array counterpart of
std::list::get: no handler needed, the default evaluated before the call,
and every other failure of at propagated. An array of affine elements cannot
use it (its at copies the element out): swap the element out instead.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::from_list([1, 2, 3])
[a.get(1, -1), a.get(7, -1)]
@value [2, -1]
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::mutable_vector
title: -
summary: MutableVector[T]: a growable mutable value with push, pop, take, swap, at, length and clear.
index: stdlib
keywords: vector; dynamic array; growable list; stack; mutable list; buffer
related: concept.mutable-vector concept.values stdlib.module.std::mutable_array stdlib.module.std::list

== NOTES
All members are intrinsics (no module file): `import std::mutable_vector`.
A MutableVector is a value, not a reference: `w = v` is an independent
logical copy (copy-on-write underneath). Mutations (push, pop, take,
swap, clear) change the vector in place, so their receiver must be a
place -- a local, parameter or context parameter, or a field path from
one (MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER otherwise). Its element type is fixed by its
type: pushing a value of another type is the TYPE error, and
from_list([]) makes a MutableVector[never] nothing can be pushed into --
make an empty one from a function whose declared result is
MutableVector[T].

Visit the elements with `loop x in v:` (which also turns it into a List).
The indexed operations (at, pop, take, swap) declare IndexNotFound, and
the compiler does not prove vector indices today: every call handles or
admits it. There is no equality or hash for vectors.

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_vector::from_list
title: -
summary: A new MutableVector holding the elements of a List, in order, written std::mutable_vector::from_list(xs).
index: stdlib
keywords: new vector; list to vector; create vector
related: stdlib.module.std::mutable_vector stdlib.std::mutable_vector::push concept.mutable-vector

== NOTES
The element type is the List's: from_list([1, 2]) is a
MutableVector[int]. An empty literal List gives a MutableVector[never],
which accepts no push; for an empty vector of a type, return
from_list([]) from a function declared `-> MutableVector[T]`. The List
moves into the vector (it becomes the owner of affine elements).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

fn no_ints() -> MutableVector[int]:
    std::mutable_vector::from_list([])

v = no_ints()
v.push(1)
w = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2, 3])
[v.length(), w.length()]
@value [1, 3]
@caption A typed empty vector
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_vector::length
title: -
summary: The number of elements of a MutableVector, written v.length().
index: stdlib
keywords: vector size; count; number of elements; len
related: stdlib.std::mutable_vector::empty? stdlib.std::list::length

== NOTES
Never fails. Unlike std::list::length it does not make v.at(i) provable: the
compiler does not relate vector indices to the length today.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2])
v.push(3)
v.length()
@value 3
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_vector::empty?
title: -
summary: Whether a MutableVector has no elements, written v.empty?().
index: stdlib
keywords: is empty; no elements; vector empty
related: stdlib.std::mutable_vector::length stdlib.std::mutable_vector::pop

== NOTES
The same as v.length() == 0; an observation that never fails.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1])
before = v.empty?()
v.clear()
[before, v.empty?()]
@value [false, true]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_vector::element
title: -
summary: The place of element i of a MutableVector an installed context owns, written c.member.element(i): observed where it is stored, never a value; IndexNotFound without element i.
index: stdlib
keywords: element place; observe element; context-owned vector; affine element; session
related: stdlib.std::mutable_vector::at stdlib.std::mutable_vector::take stdlib.std::mutable_vector::pop concept.context-owned-state error.affine-context-copy-out error.mutable-place-receiver

== NOTES
The receiver must be a place of an installed context (a context
parameter's member, `c.sessions.element(0)`): any other collection's
elements are read with at and moved out with take, pop or swap
(MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER otherwise). The result is the element's place,
used where it is written: `c.sessions.element(0).window.null?()` observes
an affine field where the vector stores it, `c.sessions.element(0).id`
reads an unrestricted one. Binding the place to a name, passing or
returning it is AFFINE-CONTEXT-COPY-OUT for an affine element (the vector
still owns it). A missing element fails with IndexNotFound, which a
handler of the call that uses the place does not cover (it is an argument
of that call): use the place in a function that declares IndexNotFound.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

struct Job:
    name: int
    weight: int

opaque context struct Queue:
    jobs: MutableVector[Job]

fn weight_of(i: int, context q: Queue) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    q.jobs.element(i).weight

fn weight(i: int) -> int:
    w = weight_of(i):
        on IndexNotFound:
            -1
    w

with context Queue {jobs: std::mutable_vector::from_list([Job {name: 1, weight: 3}])}
[weight(0), weight(1)]
@caption An element read where the installed vector stores it
@value [3, -1]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_vector::at
title: -
summary: The element of a MutableVector at index i, written v.at(i); an index outside 0..length-1 fails with IndexNotFound.
index: stdlib
keywords: vector index; element at; read element
related: stdlib.std::list::at stdlib.std::mutable_vector::take concept.mutable-vector rule.unhandled-errors

== NOTES
Every call must handle IndexNotFound or admit it: unlike std::list::at, a
vector read is not proven in range, even inside
`loop i from 0 to v.length():`. To visit every element, `loop x in v:`
needs no index at all. For a vector of affine elements at would copy
one out and is rejected (AFFINE-ELEMENT-COPY-OUT): use take, pop or swap --
or, for a vector an installed context owns, std::mutable_vector::element,
the element's place.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

fn second(v: MutableVector[int]) -> int:
    x = v.at(1):
        on IndexNotFound:
            -1
    x

[
    second(std::mutable_vector::from_list([4, 5])),
    second(std::mutable_vector::from_list([4]))
]
@value [5, -1]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_vector::push
title: -
summary: Appends x to the end of a MutableVector in place, written v.push(x); returns unit.
index: stdlib
keywords: append; add element; push back; grow; insert at end
related: stdlib.std::mutable_vector::pop stdlib.std::list::append concept.mutable-vector

== NOTES
Never fails. The receiver must be a place, and x must be admissible to
the vector's element type (a value of another type is the compile-time
TYPE error: elements are never widened or checked at run time). An
affine x moves into the vector.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1])
v.push(2)
v.push(3)
loop x in v:
    x
@value [1, 2, 3]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_vector::pop
title: -
summary: Removes and returns the last element of a MutableVector, written v.pop(); an empty vector fails with IndexNotFound and is unchanged.
index: stdlib
keywords: remove last; pop back; stack pop; take last
related: stdlib.std::mutable_vector::push stdlib.std::mutable_vector::take

== NOTES
The receiver must be a place. IndexNotFound is the empty case; it is
never proven away by the compiler, so a pop handles or admits it (test
v.empty?() first if that is clearer, but the handler is still needed).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

fn last_or_zero(v: MutableVector[int]) -> int:
    x = v.pop():
        on IndexNotFound:
            0
    x

[
    last_or_zero(std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2])),
    last_or_zero(std::mutable_vector::from_list([7]))
]
@value [2, 7]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_vector::take
title: -
summary: Removes and returns the element at index i of a MutableVector, later elements keeping their order, written v.take(i); a bad index fails with IndexNotFound.
index: stdlib
keywords: remove at; delete element; extract; remove index
related: stdlib.std::mutable_vector::pop stdlib.std::mutable_vector::swap

== NOTES
The vector becomes one element shorter; nothing changes when the index
is outside 0..length-1. The receiver must be a place. A way to move an
affine element out.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list
import std::mutable_vector

fn demo() -> List[int] errors IndexNotFound:
    v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2, 3])
    mid = v.take(1)
    rest = loop x in v:
        x
    rest.append(mid)

demo():
    on IndexNotFound:
        []
@value [1, 3, 2]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_vector::swap
title: -
summary: Stores x at index i of a MutableVector and returns the element it replaced, written v.swap(i, x); a bad index fails with IndexNotFound and changes nothing.
index: stdlib
keywords: exchange; replace element; update element; set element
related: stdlib.std::mutable_array::swap stdlib.std::mutable_vector::take

== NOTES
The vector's write operation: there is no separate set, so to overwrite
an element swap it and discard the old one. The receiver must be a place;
an affine x moves in, the old element moves out (and an affine x is
released if the index is bad).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

fn demo() -> List[int] errors IndexNotFound:
    v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2, 3])
    old = v.swap(0, 10)
    loop x in v:
        x + old

demo():
    on IndexNotFound:
        []
@value [11, 3, 4]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_vector::clear
title: -
summary: Removes every element of a MutableVector in place, written v.clear(); returns unit.
index: stdlib
keywords: empty vector; remove all; reset; truncate
related: stdlib.std::mutable_vector::empty? stdlib.std::mutable_vector::pop

== NOTES
Never fails; the receiver must be a place. Affine elements are released,
first to last. Other logical copies of the vector keep their elements.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2])
w = v
v.clear()
[v.length(), w.length()]
@value [0, 2]
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::immutable_set
title: -
summary: ImmutableSet[T]: a set fixed at construction, built from a List and queried for membership.
index: stdlib
keywords: set; membership; lookup table; distinct values
related: concept.immutable-set stdlib.==

== NOTES
Two intrinsics and no module file: from_list and contains. A set has no
order, no size, no iteration (`loop x in s:` is the TYPE error when it
runs), no union or other set algebra and no way to add or remove a
member. Sets compare with == as sets and hash consistently. Membership
uses ==, so lookup is linear in the set's size today.

@topic stdlib.std::immutable_set::from_list
title: -
summary: The ImmutableSet of the elements of a List, duplicates removed by ==, written std::immutable_set::from_list(xs).
index: stdlib
keywords: make set; list to set; deduplicate; unique values
related: stdlib.std::immutable_set::contains concept.immutable-set stdlib.std::byte::set

== NOTES
Deduplicates with ==, so members need equality: two functions or arrays
that must be compared fail with the EQUALITY error. Its type is
ImmutableSet[T] for a List[T]. Built once and kept (for example as a
module value), it can serve many contains calls.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::immutable_set

a = std::immutable_set::from_list([1, 2, 2])
b = std::immutable_set::from_list([2, 1])
a == b
@value true
@caption Order and duplicates do not matter
@end

@topic stdlib.std::immutable_set::contains
title: -
summary: Whether a value is a member of an ImmutableSet (by ==), written s.contains(v); total: an absent value or one of another kind is false.
index: stdlib
keywords: member; membership test; in set; includes; lookup
related: stdlib.std::immutable_set::from_list stdlib.==

== NOTES
Never fails for a set: a value of a different kind than the members
simply is not one. Equality is ==, so a member that is a function or an
array makes the comparison the EQUALITY error.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::immutable_set

vowels = std::immutable_set::from_list(['a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'])
[vowels.contains('e'), vowels.contains('z'), vowels.contains(1)]
@value [true, false, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::coroutine
title: -
summary: Observing coroutine handles: std::coroutine::done? (the coroutine binding, yield and resuming are syntax, not functions).
index: stdlib
keywords: coroutine functions; generator; resumable
related: concept.coroutines concept.affine

== NOTES
The namespace has one public intrinsic, std::coroutine::done?, and no module
file. A coroutine is made by the `coroutine {step, first} = f(args)`
binding, suspends at `yield`, and is resumed by calling its handle
(concept.coroutines); the operations behind them are internal
coroutine#... natives no source can name.

@topic stdlib.std::coroutine::done?
title: -
summary: Whether a coroutine has finished -- completed or failed -- written step.done?() after `import std::coroutine`; it does not consume the handle.
index: stdlib
keywords: finished; is complete; generator exhausted; has more
related: concept.coroutines concept.affine stdlib.module.std::coroutine

== NOTES
True once the coroutine has returned or failed with an unhandled error;
false while it is suspended at a yield. It only observes the handle: the
handle stays usable afterwards (resuming a finished coroutine runs no
code and returns its cached result again, or raises its cached error).
It runs on every backend; natively, coroutines exist on Linux x86-64
and AArch64.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::coroutine

fn countdown(n: int) -> int:
    loop i from 0 to n:
        yield i
    n

coroutine {step, first} = countdown(2)
a = step.done?()
second = step()
third = step()
[first, a, second, third, step.done?()]
@value [0, false, 1, 2, true]
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::byte_store
title: -
summary: The immutable byte storage behind std::abi::bytes::Bytes: build one from a List of bytes and measure it (a substrate: use std::abi::bytes).
index: stdlib
keywords: byte storage; raw bytes; byte buffer
related: stdlib.module.std::abi::bytes stdlib.module.std::mutable_byte_store

== NOTES
Two intrinsics, no module file. A byte storage is a runtime value kind of
its own: an immutable, finite sequence of bytes with an exact length, no
terminator, compared and hashed by its bytes. No source type spells it (its
static type is any); std::abi::bytes::Bytes is the opaque struct whose one
field holds one, and only module std::abi::bytes can construct or open a
Bytes. So programs use std::abi::bytes::from_list and
std::abi::bytes::length; these intrinsics are what that module is written
in. There is deliberately no operation that reads, slices or iterates the
bytes.

@topic stdlib.std::byte_store::from_list
title: -
summary: A new immutable byte storage holding exactly the given byte values, in order; every element must be an Int in 0..255.
index: stdlib
keywords: bytes from list; make bytes; byte buffer
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::from_list stdlib.std::str::encode_utf8 stdlib.module.std::byte_store

== NOTES
The substrate of std::abi::bytes::from_list, which is what programs call (it
takes a List[std::byte::Byte], so its elements are proven bytes). The
conversion is eager: the storage owns its own copy and does not retain
the List. Each element is stored exactly -- no `mod 256`, no signed
reinterpretation -- and a 0 is an ordinary byte. An element that is not
an Int in 0..255 is the TYPE error when the call runs ("element 1 must be
a byte"). Context-free: usable in a module value.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::byte_store
import std::str

s = std::byte_store::from_list("hi!".encode_utf8())
s.byte_count()
@value 3
@end

@topic stdlib.std::byte_store::byte_count
title: -
summary: The exact number of bytes of an immutable byte storage.
index: stdlib
keywords: byte length; size in bytes; count bytes
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::length stdlib.module.std::byte_store

== NOTES
Never fails for a storage (another kind is the TYPE error). Programs ask
std::abi::bytes::length(data), which returns the same count as a
std::abi::Usize.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::byte_store

std::byte_store::from_list([]).byte_count()
@value 0
@end

@topic stdlib.std::byte_store::join
title: -
summary: One new immutable byte storage holding the bytes of every storage of a List, in order.
index: stdlib
keywords: concatenate bytes; join bytes; append bytes; combine byte buffers
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::join stdlib.module.std::byte_store

== NOTES
The substrate of std::abi::bytes::join, which is what programs call (it
takes a List[std::abi::bytes::Bytes]). One copy of the total length; the
empty List, or only empty parts, gives the empty storage. An element that
is not a byte storage is the TYPE error when the call runs. It reads no
byte into a Botlish value. WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-7.md.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::byte_store
import std::str

a = std::byte_store::from_list("ab".encode_utf8())
std::byte_store::join([a, a]).byte_count()
@value 4
@end

@topic stdlib.std::byte_store::slice
title: -
summary: The byte storage of a storage's bytes from index start up to but not including end; an invalid range fails with LowerUnderrun or UpperOverrun.
index: stdlib
keywords: byte slice; part of bytes; rest of a buffer; LowerUnderrun; UpperOverrun
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::slice stdlib.std::byte_store::join stdlib.module.std::byte_store proof.slices

== NOTES
The substrate of std::abi::bytes::slice, which is what programs call (it
takes and returns a std::abi::bytes::Bytes). The range is half-open over
byte indices, valid exactly when 0 <= start <= end <= the byte count, with
std::str::substring's rule for the two errors: start below 0, or end below
start, is LowerUnderrun; end past the count is UpperOverrun. It copies the
range into a new storage and reads no byte into a Botlish value. The file
writers use it to continue after a short write
(WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-7.md).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::byte_store
import std::str

part = std::byte_store::from_list("abcdef".encode_utf8()).slice(1, 4):
    on LowerUnderrun:
        std::byte_store::from_list([])
    on UpperOverrun:
        std::byte_store::from_list([])
part.byte_count()
@value 3
@end

@topic stdlib.std::byte_store::decode_utf8
title: -
summary: The String whose UTF-8 encoding is a byte storage's bytes, or the declared error InvalidUtf8.
index: stdlib
keywords: decode utf-8; bytes to string; utf8 decode; text from bytes; InvalidUtf8
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::decode_utf8 stdlib.std::str::encode_utf8 stdlib.module.std::byte_store

== NOTES
The substrate of std::abi::bytes::decode_utf8, which is what programs call
(it takes a std::abi::bytes::Bytes). Strict: an overlong form, an encoded
surrogate (WTF-8's form of an unpaired UTF-16 surrogate), a value above
U+10FFFF, a truncated sequence or a stray continuation byte is the declared
error InvalidUtf8, never a replacement character. A 0 byte is U+0000.
WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-7.md.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::byte_store
import std::str

std::byte_store::from_list("hé".encode_utf8()).decode_utf8():
    on InvalidUtf8:
        "not text"
@value "hé"
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::mutable_byte_store
title: -
summary: The writable byte storage behind std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes: fixed-length byte sequences whose updates return fresh storage (a substrate: use std::abi::bytes).
index: stdlib
keywords: mutable bytes; writable buffer; byte buffer
related: stdlib.module.std::abi::bytes stdlib.module.std::byte_store

== NOTES
Seven intrinsics, no module file: the operations stdlib/abi/bytes.bot builds
std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes from. A mutable byte storage is a value: no
operation here ever changes a storage another value can observe --
replace returns a new storage, and detach makes a physical copy for a
foreign write. Its length is fixed. None of them is context-free, so a
module value cannot hold one. Programs normally use std::abi::bytes::zeroed,
from_bytes, mutable_length, replace, detach, freeze and freeze_prefix,
whose errors are declared (IndexNotFound, UpperOverrun) where these
intrinsics fail with run-time RANGE errors.

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::zeroed
title: -
summary: A new writable byte storage of n zero bytes.
index: stdlib
keywords: zero buffer; allocate bytes; new buffer
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::zeroed stdlib.module.std::mutable_byte_store

== NOTES
Nothing is ever uninitialized: every byte is 0. n must be 0..2^62-1 (the
run-time RANGE error otherwise); the Tcl backends also refuse more than
2^30 bytes with RANGE. Programs call std::abi::bytes::zeroed(length), which
takes a std::abi::Usize.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_byte_store

z = std::mutable_byte_store::zeroed(4)
z.count()
@value 4
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::from_storage
title: -
summary: A writable byte storage holding a copy of an immutable byte storage's bytes; changing it never changes the original.
index: stdlib
keywords: writable copy; thaw; bytes to mutable
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::from_bytes stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::freeze

== NOTES
The substrate of std::abi::bytes::from_bytes. The argument must be an
immutable byte storage (another kind is the TYPE error).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::byte_store
import std::mutable_byte_store
import std::str

s = std::byte_store::from_list("hi".encode_utf8())
std::mutable_byte_store::from_storage(s).count()
@value 2
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::count
title: -
summary: The fixed number of bytes of a writable byte storage.
index: stdlib
keywords: byte length; size in bytes; count bytes
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::mutable_length

== NOTES
Never fails for a writable storage. Programs ask
std::abi::bytes::mutable_length(data).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_byte_store

std::mutable_byte_store::zeroed(3).count()
@value 3
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::replace
title: -
summary: A new writable byte storage equal to m except that byte i is b, written m.replace(i, b); m itself is never written.
index: stdlib
keywords: set byte; write byte; update byte; poke
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::replace stdlib.module.std::mutable_byte_store

== NOTES
An update returns a fresh storage (natively a copy, then the write), so
every other holder of m keeps the old bytes. An index outside
0..count-1 is the run-time RANGE error and a value outside 0..255 the
TYPE error. std::abi::bytes::replace is the checked form programs use: its
Byte parameter proves the value, and a bad index is its declared error
IndexNotFound.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::byte_store
import std::mutable_byte_store
import std::str

s = std::byte_store::from_list("hi!".encode_utf8())
m = std::mutable_byte_store::from_storage(s)
m2 = m.replace(0, 72)
[
    m2.freeze() == std::byte_store::from_list("Hi!".encode_utf8()),
    m.freeze() == s
]
@value [true, true]
@caption The update is a new value
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::detach
title: -
summary: A writable byte storage equal to m with its own freshly allocated bytes: semantically m itself, physically a copy.
index: stdlib
keywords: copy buffer; detach; clone bytes
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::detach stdlib.std::abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes

== NOTES
Copy-on-write's detach, taken every time: what a caller does before
handing the storage to a foreign write (std::linux::read), because the
writable address bridge writes through the storage it is given. As a
value it is indistinguishable from m (== is true).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_byte_store

m = std::mutable_byte_store::zeroed(2)
m.detach() == m
@value true
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::freeze
title: -
summary: An immutable byte storage holding a copy of a writable storage's current bytes.
index: stdlib
keywords: snapshot; freeze bytes; mutable to immutable
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::freeze stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::freeze_prefix

== NOTES
The snapshot never changes afterwards, and m stays a valid, independent
value. Programs call std::abi::bytes::freeze(data), which returns a Bytes.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::byte_store
import std::mutable_byte_store

std::mutable_byte_store::zeroed(2).freeze().byte_count()
@value 2
@end

@topic stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::freeze_prefix
title: -
summary: An immutable byte storage holding a copy of the first n bytes of a writable storage, written m.freeze_prefix(n).
index: stdlib
keywords: prefix; first bytes; truncate snapshot
related: stdlib.std::abi::bytes::freeze_prefix stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::freeze

== NOTES
For the bytes a read(2) that returned n actually wrote. n outside
0..count is the run-time RANGE error; std::abi::bytes::freeze_prefix checks
the count first and fails with its declared UpperOverrun instead.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::byte_store
import std::mutable_byte_store

z = std::mutable_byte_store::zeroed(4)
z.freeze_prefix(1).byte_count()
@value 1
@end

@topic stdlib.std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes
title: -
summary: The machine address of a std::abi::bytes::Bytes payload as an x86-64 register word, for a raw syscall; native backends only (interp and compile fail with NATIVE-ONLY).
index: stdlib
keywords: buffer address; pointer to bytes; raw address; syscall argument
related: backend.native-only stdlib.std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall stdlib.std::abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes stdlib.module.std::abi::bytes

== NOTES
Returns a std::abi::x86_64::Register64 holding the address of the first byte
of the Bytes' storage (a valid one-past address for an empty one): the
value a register argument of std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall carries. The
argument must statically be a std::abi::bytes::Bytes, and the bridge is only
ever called: binding it as a function value is a TYPE error ("can only be
called directly"). The native lowering keeps the storage alive until the
syscall that consumes the address has returned. The address is read-only
for the kernel: a buffer it writes needs from_mutable_bytes.

A machine address exists only on the native backends (cranelift,
cranelift-generic, executables) on Linux x86-64; the Tcl backends refuse the
call when it runs (NATIVE-ONLY) and imitate nothing. It is not a direct
operation by itself -- the syscall that uses the address is. Ordinary
programs never need it: std::linux::write and std::io::print_line call it
for them.

@topic stdlib.std::abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes
title: -
summary: The machine address of a std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes payload as an x86-64 register word, for a syscall that writes the buffer; native backends only (NATIVE-ONLY elsewhere).
index: stdlib
keywords: writable buffer address; raw address; read buffer
related: backend.native-only stdlib.std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes stdlib.std::mutable_byte_store::detach

== NOTES
The writable twin of std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes: the same contract, but
the argument must statically be a std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes and the
kernel may write through the address. It does not copy: it hands out the
address of the storage it is given, so the caller must pass storage no other
value observes -- std::linux::read does, by detaching a private copy first
(std::abi::bytes::detach) and returning that copy as the result's data. That
is the raw layer's sharp edge; use std::linux::read or std::linux::io
instead.

@topic stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::from_bytes
title: -
summary: The machine address of a std::abi::bytes::Bytes payload as an AArch64 register word, for a raw syscall; native backends only (NATIVE-ONLY elsewhere).
index: stdlib
keywords: buffer address; raw address; syscall argument; arm64
related: backend.native-only stdlib.std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes stdlib.std::linux::abi::aarch64::syscall

== NOTES
std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes's AArch64 row: the same contract, returning an
std::abi::aarch64::Register64 for std::linux::abi::aarch64::syscall's
registers. One architecture's register word never reaches the other's
transport. Runs only on the native backends on Linux AArch64.

@topic stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::from_mutable_bytes
title: -
summary: The machine address of a std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes payload as an AArch64 register word, for a syscall that writes the buffer; native backends only.
index: stdlib
keywords: writable buffer address; raw address; arm64
related: backend.native-only stdlib.std::abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::from_bytes

== NOTES
std::abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes's AArch64 row: the writable bridge,
with the same no-copy sharp edge, returning a std::abi::aarch64::Register64.
Runs only on the native backends on Linux AArch64.

@topic stdlib.module.std::linux::abi::x86_64
title: -
summary: The raw Linux x86-64 syscall transport, std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall: native backends only, and a direct namespace (raw authority).
index: stdlib
keywords: syscall; system call; kernel; x86-64
related: concept.direct-modules backend.native-only stdlib.module.std::linux

== NOTES
An intrinsic namespace declared direct (DIRECT-MODULES.md): a module that
calls into it must itself be a `direct module` or mediate the call through a
context it declares. stdlib/linux/sys.x86_64.bot and stdlib/linux.bot wrap
it; programs use std::linux::write, std::linux::read, std::linux::io or io.

@topic stdlib.module.std::linux::abi::aarch64
title: -
summary: The raw Linux AArch64 syscall transport (svc #0), std::linux::abi::aarch64::syscall: native backends only, and a direct namespace (raw authority).
index: stdlib
keywords: syscall; system call; kernel; arm64; aarch64
related: concept.direct-modules backend.native-only stdlib.module.std::linux

== NOTES
The AArch64 row of the transport table; see
stdlib.module.std::linux::abi::x86_64. stdlib/linux/sys.aarch64.bot wraps it
on an AArch64 target.

@topic stdlib.std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall
title: -
summary: One raw Linux x86-64 kernel transition: the registers {rax, rdi, rsi, rdx, r10, r8, r9} in, the raw rax back; native backends only.
index: stdlib
keywords: syscall; system call; kernel call; raw syscall; x86-64
related: backend.native-only concept.direct-modules stdlib.module.std::linux stdlib.std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes

== NOTES
The argument is an anonymous struct whose fields are named after the x86-64
Linux syscall registers and hold std::abi::x86_64::Register64 values: rax
(the syscall number, required) and rdi, rsi, rdx, r10, r8, r9 (arguments
1-6; an omitted one is zero). Another field is UNKNOWN-FIELD, a missing rax
MISSING-FIELD, and a field of another type (an AArch64 register included)
the TYPE error. It executes `syscall` and returns rax as a Register64: the
kernel's raw result, with no errno decoding, no -1 convention, no retry.
Read it with std::abi::x86_64::to_int (a negative value is -errno).

The transport knows the register convention and nothing about any
syscall: numbers and meanings are source data (stdlib/linux/sys.x86_64.bot).
It is never context-free, folded, merged, reordered or removed.

Native backends only (cranelift, cranelift-generic, executables), and only
on an x86-64 host (elsewhere NATIVE UNSUPPORTED); interp and compile refuse
the call when it runs with NATIVE-ONLY. The namespace is direct: the calling
module must be a `direct module` or mediate the call through a context of
its own. Ordinary programs use std::linux::write, std::linux::read,
std::linux::io or io instead.

@topic stdlib.std::linux::abi::aarch64::syscall
title: -
summary: One raw Linux AArch64 kernel transition (svc #0): the registers {x8, x0, ..., x5} in, the raw x0 back; native backends only.
index: stdlib
keywords: syscall; system call; kernel call; raw syscall; arm64; aarch64
related: backend.native-only stdlib.std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall stdlib.module.std::linux

== NOTES
Exactly the x86-64 transport's contract on AArch64: x8 the syscall number
(required), x0..x5 arguments 1-6 (omitted ones zero), every field an
std::abi::aarch64::Register64, the result the raw x0. It runs only on the
native backends on an AArch64 host; interp and compile refuse it with
NATIVE-ONLY. stdlib/linux/sys.aarch64.bot holds the AArch64 syscall numbers.

@topic stdlib.module.std::binary
title: -
summary: Fixed binary layouts: parse, emit and field writes of byte records (std::binary::parse, emit, write, write_foreign), native views and edits (load, store, element, store_element), and layout queries.
index: stdlib
keywords: binary format; struct packing; serialize; deserialize; byte layout; parse bytes
related: concept.binary-layouts stdlib.module.std::abi::bytes

== NOTES
`import std::binary` for the ten intrinsics and the scalar field types
std::binary::U8 ... std::binary::I64. A `layout` declaration names a fixed
number of bytes with explicit size, alignment and field offsets; every
multi-byte field is little-endian (the target's order), and padding is never
a field. The layout is static: it is named at a parse or a query and read
from the value's type at an emit, never a runtime value. A field may be a
fixed array, `field name: array 16 of std::ffi::c::Char at 8`: N elements of
any representation (a scalar, a pointer, a nested layout, another array),
element i at the field's offset plus i times the element's width; its
copied value is a List of exactly N elements (FIXED-LAYOUT-ARRAYS.md).
There are no bitfields, variable-length tails or zero-length arrays in a
layout; overlapping interpretations are a union layout's (LAYOUT-UNIONS.md).

A field may be C's float or double (std::ffi::c::Float, 4 bytes;
std::ffi::c::Double, 8), its value a Float64. A read of one --
std::binary::parse, std::binary::read, std::binary::load,
std::binary::element, a view's field -- fails with the declared
NonFiniteFloat when its bits are a NaN or an infinity (negative zero reads
as 0.0); a write, store or emit of a float rounds the Float64 to nearest,
ties to even, and fails with NonFiniteFloat when it overflows the float,
before a byte is written. A call is charged NonFiniteFloat only when it
reads a float or a double or writes a float: the errors listed below include
it for those calls alone (concept.c-floats).

A field may be a C pointer (std::ffi::c::Ptr[T] or std::ffi::c::FnPtr[F], of
the target's pointer width). Such a layout is native-only: bytes never
become a pointer, so std::binary::parse and std::binary::emit of it are
LAYOUT-NATIVE-ONLY, and it is read from live native memory through a
NativeView[L] (std::binary::native::view, then its fields or
std::binary::load).

Writes name their field statically, LAYOUT.FIELD or a path through nested
layouts (Header.origin.x). A record in byte storage the program owns is
filled a field at a time: std::binary::write(data, L.f, v) is a new
std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes with that field written (data unchanged), and
a pointer field takes an existing pointer only through the explicit
std::binary::write_foreign. Live native memory foreign code handed out is
written through a NativeEdit[L] (std::binary::native::edit, then
std::binary::store). NATIVE-LAYOUT-WRITES.md.

A fixed array read through a view or an edit (v.items) is a live array,
NativeView[array N of E] or NativeEdit[array N of E]:
std::binary::element(a, i) reads one element at a dynamic index
(IndexNotFound outside 0..N-1, no failure when the index is proven),
std::binary::store_element(e.items, i, v) writes one scalar or pointer
element, and std::binary::load(a) copies the whole array into its List. A
designator names an element statically too (L.items[3], L.m[1][2],
L.pts[2].x), and a whole array is written from a List of exactly its extent
(the declared LayoutExtentMismatch otherwise, before anything is written).

@topic stdlib.std::binary::parse
title: -
summary: The LayoutStruct[L] imprinted from bytes of exactly layout L's size, written std::binary::parse(L, data); any other length fails with LayoutLengthMismatch.
index: stdlib
keywords: parse bytes; decode; read struct; deserialize; unpack
related: concept.binary-layouts stdlib.std::binary::emit stdlib.std::abi::bytes::from_list rule.unhandled-errors

== NOTES
L is a layout name written at the call (qualified for another module's
layout), never a value; data must statically be a std::abi::bytes::Bytes.
Each field is read at its offset in its encoding (nested layouts
recursively); padding bytes are ignored. The result holds the fields only
-- no view of or reference to the input.

Fewer or more bytes than L's size is the builtin declared error
LayoutLengthMismatch, and the compiler does not prove it away from the
Bytes' length today: every call handles or admits it. There is no prefix
parse and no cursor. Declared nomethod: the layout comes first and is no
receiver (Header.parse(d) is rejected).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary
import std::str

layout Pair:
    size 2
    align 1
    field a: std::binary::U8 at 0
    field b: std::binary::U8 at 1

fn second(data: std::abi::bytes::Bytes) -> int:
    p = std::binary::parse(Pair, data):
        on LayoutLengthMismatch:
            return -1
    p.b

[
    second(std::abi::bytes::from_list("AB".encode_utf8())),
    second(std::abi::bytes::from_list("ABC".encode_utf8()))
]
@value [66, -1]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::binary::emit
title: -
summary: The bytes of a LayoutStruct[L] value laid out by L, written v.emit(): a Bytes of L's size, padding zero; fails only when a fixed array's List has another length (LayoutExtentMismatch).
index: stdlib
keywords: serialize; encode; write struct; pack; to bytes
related: concept.binary-layouts stdlib.std::binary::parse

== NOTES
L is read from the value's static type, which must be LayoutStruct[L] (an
untyped value or an ordinary struct is BINARY-OPERAND). Every field is
written at its offset in its encoding, little-endian, and padding is written
as zero bytes, so equal values emit equal bytes. A field's value is in its
domain by its type; a fixed array's List must have exactly the array's
extent (its type cannot say so), else the call fails with the declared
LayoutExtentMismatch and emits nothing -- unless the compiler proves every
extent (a list literal, a counted loop, a value std::binary::parse or
std::binary::load made), when it declares no error. A layout without arrays
never fails. parse of emit's result gives the value back. A LayoutStruct is
built from its fields with `LayoutStruct[L] {field: value, ...}`. It is
never a function value.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary
import std::str

layout Pair:
    size 3
    align 1
    field a: std::binary::U16 at 0
    field b: std::binary::U8 at 2

p = LayoutStruct[Pair] {a: 16706, b: 67}
p.emit() == std::abi::bytes::from_list("BAC".encode_utf8())
@value true
@caption 16706 is 0x4142, little-endian
@end

@topic stdlib.std::binary::native::view
title: -
summary: The read-only NativeView[L] of the native memory a data pointer addresses, written std::binary::native::view(L, p); a null p fails with NativeViewNull.
index: stdlib
keywords: native view; read native memory; read C struct; peek; overlay; pointer to struct; view record
related: concept.binary-layouts stdlib.std::binary::load stdlib.module.std::binary concept.c-ffi rule.unhandled-errors

== NOTES
L is a layout name written at the call; p must statically be an
std::ffi::c::Ptr[T] (a data pointer), observed, never moved. Nothing is read
when the view is made: each field is read when it is projected (v.field,
one load at the layout's offset), a nested layout's field is a
NativeView of the inner layout, and a pointer field is the exact pointer
the memory holds. A view is read-only, never a pointer, a LayoutStruct or
another layout's view, and has no equality. Making one is raw authority:
std::binary::native is a direct namespace, so the calling module must be a
`direct module` (or mediate the call through a context of its own);
reading a view someone else made is not. It reads memory only on the
native backends: interp and compile refuse a view of a non-null pointer
with NATIVE-ONLY. A null pointer is the builtin declared error
NativeViewNull on every backend. The memory must hold at least
std::binary::layout_length(L) bytes for as long as the view is read; nothing
checks it. Declared nomethod. NATIVE-LAYOUT-VIEWS.md.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
direct module

import std::binary
import std::binary::native
import std::ffi::c

layout Point:
    size 4
    align 2
    field x: std::binary::I16 at 0
    field y: std::binary::I16 at 2

fn x_of(p: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]) -> int errors NativeViewNull:
    v = std::binary::native::view(Point, p)
    v.x

r = x_of(std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Void]()):
    on NativeViewNull:
        -1
r
@value -1
@caption A view of the null pointer fails before anything is read
@end

@topic stdlib.std::binary::native::edit
title: -
summary: The NativeEdit[L] of the native memory a data pointer addresses -- read and written through layout L -- written std::binary::native::edit(L, p); a null p fails with NativeEditNull.
index: stdlib
keywords: native edit; write native memory; write C struct; poke; fill struct; out parameter; modify record
related: concept.binary-layouts stdlib.std::binary::store stdlib.std::binary::native::view stdlib.std::binary::load concept.c-ffi rule.unhandled-errors

== NOTES
L is a layout name written at the call; p must statically be an
std::ffi::c::Ptr[T] (a data pointer), observed, never moved. Nothing is read
or written when the edit is made. An edit reads exactly as a NativeView does
(v.field, destructuring, std::binary::load) except that a nested layout's
field is a NativeEdit of the inner layout, and std::binary::store writes
through it. It is write authority, never ownership: no lifetime, no
exclusive access (copies, other edits and views of the same address, and
foreign code all alias the memory). A NativeView never becomes an edit and
an edit is not a view: neither is the other's subtype. Making one is raw
authority like a view: std::binary::native is a direct namespace, so the
calling module must be a `direct module` (or mediate the call through a
context of its own); writing through an edit someone else made is not. It
touches memory only on the native backends: interp and compile refuse an
edit of a non-null pointer with NATIVE-ONLY. A null pointer is the builtin
declared error NativeEditNull on every backend. The memory must be live,
writable and at least std::binary::layout_length(L) bytes for as long as the
edit is used; nothing checks it. Declared nomethod. NATIVE-LAYOUT-WRITES.md.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
direct module

import std::binary
import std::binary::native
import std::ffi::c

layout Point:
    size 4
    align 2
    field x: std::binary::I16 at 0
    field y: std::binary::I16 at 2

fn reset(p: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]) -> int errors NativeEditNull:
    e = std::binary::native::edit(Point, p)
    std::binary::store(e, Point.x, 0)
    e.y

r = reset(std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Void]()):
    on NativeEditNull:
        -1
r
@value -1
@caption An edit of the null pointer fails before anything is written
@end

@topic stdlib.std::binary::store
title: -
summary: One field written into live native memory through a NativeEdit[L], written std::binary::store(e, L.field, value): one store of the field's width at its offset, nothing else touched.
index: stdlib
keywords: write field; store field; set native field; poke; assign struct field; write pointer field
related: stdlib.std::binary::native::edit stdlib.std::binary::write concept.binary-layouts

== NOTES
e must statically be a NativeEdit[L] (a NativeView is read-only:
BINARY-OPERAND), and the field is written L.FIELD, or a path through
nested layouts (L.origin.x), naming a field of e's own layout
(LAYOUT-OPERAND otherwise). The value is held to the field's type the way
a construction's field values are: a scalar proven in its integer domain
(no conversion, truncation or run-time guard), a pointer field exactly its
declared std::ffi::c::Ptr or FnPtr type. A pointer stored is observed, never
moved: its address bits are copied, the memory does not own the pointee,
and nothing keeps it alive. Padding and every other field are untouched.
A designator may name an array element statically (L.items[3]) or a
whole fixed array, whose value is a List of exactly its extent: every
extent is checked before the first byte is written (LayoutExtentMismatch,
declared only when the compiler cannot prove the extents). Otherwise it
declares no error and returns unit. A store is an ordinary native
store: not volatile, atomic or ordered; another thread's synchronization
is the program's protocol. Declared nomethod. NATIVE-LAYOUT-WRITES.md.

@topic stdlib.std::binary::write
title: -
summary: A new std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes with one ordinary field of layout L written, std::binary::write(data, L.field, value); data must be exactly L's size (LayoutLengthMismatch).
index: stdlib
keywords: write field into bytes; build record; fill struct in bytes; set field; serialize field; pack field
related: stdlib.std::binary::write_foreign stdlib.std::binary::store stdlib.std::binary::emit stdlib.module.std::abi::bytes concept.binary-layouts

== NOTES
data must statically be a std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes; the field is
written L.FIELD or a path through nested layouts, and its value is held to
the field's integer domain like a construction's. The result is a fresh
MutableBytes (MutableBytes is a value: data and every copy of it are
unchanged), so a record is built a write at a time from
std::abi::bytes::zeroed(std::abi::usize(std::binary::layout_length(L))). A
designator may name an array element (L.items[3]) or a whole fixed array
written from a List of exactly its extent (LayoutExtentMismatch otherwise,
checked after the size and before anything is written). A pointer field, or
an array holding pointers, is never written this way (LAYOUT-NATIVE-ONLY):
std::binary::write_foreign encodes existing pointers. Runs on every backend.
Declared nomethod.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary

layout Pair:
    size 4
    align 2
    field a: std::binary::U16 at 0
    field b: std::binary::I8 at 3

fn build() -> std::abi::bytes::Bytes errors LayoutLengthMismatch:
    d = std::abi::bytes::zeroed(std::abi::usize(std::binary::layout_length(Pair)))
    e = std::binary::write(d, Pair.a, 258)
    std::abi::bytes::freeze(std::binary::write(e, Pair.b, -1))

r = build():
    on LayoutLengthMismatch:
        std::abi::bytes::from_list([])
std::binary::parse(Pair, r):
    on LayoutLengthMismatch:
        LayoutStruct[Pair] {a: 0, b: 0}
@value LayoutStruct[Pair] {a: 258, b: -1}
@end

@topic stdlib.std::binary::read
title: -
summary: The one representation a field designator names, imprinted from bytes of exactly L's size, std::binary::read(data, L.path): a scalar, a record, an array, one alternative of a union.
index: stdlib
keywords: read field from bytes; read one field; union alternative; interpret bytes as; read alternative; pick union member
related: stdlib.std::binary::write stdlib.std::binary::parse concept.layout-unions concept.binary-layouts

== NOTES
data must statically be a std::abi::bytes::Bytes (std::abi::bytes::freeze a
MutableBytes); the designator is written L.FIELD, a path through nested
layouts and unions (L.u.alt.field) and element indices (L.items[3]),
resolved statically to one constant offset. It is std::binary::write's read
twin, and how a union is read from bytes: a union has no copied value
(LAYOUT-UNION), so a program reads the alternative it means
(std::binary::read(data, U.whole)), each alternative by its own encoding. It
may name a nested layout (its LayoutStruct) or a fixed array (a List of
exactly its extent, which the compiler knows); never a union itself
(LAYOUT-OPERAND: name an alternative), a C pointer, or anything holding
one (LAYOUT-NATIVE-ONLY: bytes never become a pointer). Runs on every
backend. Declared nomethod. LAYOUT-UNIONS.md.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary

union layout WORD:
    size 4
    align 4
    alternative whole: std::binary::U32
    alternative bytes: array 4 of std::binary::U8

fn f(d: std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes) -> list errors LayoutLengthMismatch:
    e = std::abi::bytes::freeze(std::binary::write(d, WORD.whole, 16909060))
    [std::binary::read(e, WORD.whole), std::binary::read(e, WORD.bytes), std::binary::read(e, WORD.bytes[3])]

r = f(std::abi::bytes::zeroed(std::abi::usize(4))):
    on LayoutLengthMismatch:
        []
r
@value [16909060, [4, 3, 2, 1], 1]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::binary::write_foreign
title: -
summary: A new std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes with an existing C pointer's address bits encoded into a pointer field of layout L, std::binary::write_foreign(data, L.field, p).
index: stdlib
keywords: write pointer into bytes; encode pointer; build C struct; pointer field; callback field; userdata field
related: stdlib.std::binary::write stdlib.std::binary::store concept.c-ffi concept.binary-layouts

== NOTES
The explicit foreign encoding: the field must be a C pointer field, or a
fixed array of them written from a List of pointers of exactly its extent
(LAYOUT-FOREIGN-WRITE otherwise: an array of records holding pointers is
filled a field at a time, L.rs[1].p), and p exactly its declared
std::ffi::c::Ptr or FnPtr type (an Int is TYPE). p is observed, never moved:
the bytes do not own it, keep it alive, or know whether C keeps it after a
call -- a wrapper models that protocol. The record built this way is exposed
to C by the existing borrowing contract (a read noescape pointer parameter).
The law is one-way: std::binary::parse of a layout with a pointer field
stays LAYOUT-NATIVE-ONLY whatever the bytes hold, and a pointer is read back
only from native memory, through a view or an edit. Runs on every backend (a
C pointer's address is its value there too). Declared nomethod.
NATIVE-LAYOUT-WRITES.md.

@topic stdlib.std::binary::load
title: -
summary: Every field of a native view read once into a LayoutStruct[L], written std::binary::load(v) or v.load(), L from the view's static type NativeView[L].
index: stdlib
keywords: read whole struct; copy native record; snapshot; load view
related: concept.binary-layouts stdlib.std::binary::native::view stdlib.std::binary::parse

== NOTES
v must statically be a NativeView[L] or a NativeEdit[L] (anything else is
BINARY-OPERAND) -- or a live fixed array, NativeView[array N of E] (v.items,
or an element of one), whose copy is its List of exactly N elements (rows a
List of Lists, records a List of LayoutStructs). The result is the same
immutable LayoutStruct[L] std::binary::parse would give for the same bytes,
nested layouts and arrays included, so a record read many times can be
loaded once and read as ordinary fields; padding is never read. For a
native-only layout the LayoutStruct owns the pointers it read and is affine
like any value that holds a Ptr. Natively only, like the view itself.

@topic stdlib.std::binary::element
title: -
summary: Element i of a live fixed array, written std::binary::element(a, i): a scalar's Int, a pointer, or a nested layout's or row's own live view or edit; IndexNotFound unless 0 <= i < N.
index: stdlib
keywords: array element; index native array; read element; c array; subscript
related: stdlib.std::binary::store_element stdlib.std::binary::load stdlib.std::binary::native::view concept.binary-layouts rule.unhandled-errors

== NOTES
a must statically be a live fixed array, NativeView[array N of E] or
NativeEdit[array N of E] (an array field read through a view or an edit,
v.items, or an element of one); anything else is BINARY-OPERAND. One
access at the array's address plus i times the element's width: a scalar
element is one load, a pointer element is read fresh (observed, affine
like any pointer), and a record or row element is its own live view
(edit, through an edit) at its address, nothing read. The whole array is
never copied. An index the compiler proves in 0..N-1 (a constant, a loop
over the extent, a branch) needs no handler; one proven outside is
KNOWN-ERROR. It reads native memory only on the native backends (a view
of a non-null pointer is refused on interp and compile). Declared
nomethod. FIXED-LAYOUT-ARRAYS.md.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
direct module

import std::binary
import std::binary::native
import std::ffi::c

layout Name:
    size 16
    align 1
    field chars: array 16 of std::ffi::c::UChar at 0

fn first_two(p: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]) -> int errors NativeViewNull:
    v = std::binary::native::view(Name, p)
    std::binary::element(v.chars, 0) + std::binary::element(v.chars, 1)

r = first_two(std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Void]()):
    on NativeViewNull:
        -1
r
@value -1
@caption Proven indices need no handler; a view of null fails first
@end

@topic stdlib.std::binary::store_element
title: -
summary: One scalar or pointer element of a live fixed array written through a native edit, std::binary::store_element(e.items, i, v): one store at the element's offset; IndexNotFound unless 0 <= i < N.
index: stdlib
keywords: write array element; store element; set native array; c array assignment
related: stdlib.std::binary::element stdlib.std::binary::store stdlib.std::binary::native::edit concept.binary-layouts

== NOTES
The array must statically be NativeEdit[array N of E] (an array read through
a view is read-only: BINARY-OPERAND) and its element a scalar or a pointer
(a record or row element is LAYOUT-OPERAND: write its fields through
std::binary::element(a, i), the element's own edit). The value is held to
the element's type as std::binary::store's is. Exactly the element's bytes
are written; a store outside 0..N-1 writes nothing. A static index is also a
designator: std::binary::store(e, L.items[3], v). Declared nomethod.
FIXED-LAYOUT-ARRAYS.md.

@topic stdlib.std::binary::layout_length
title: -
summary: The size in bytes of layout L, written std::binary::layout_length(L): a compile-time constant.
index: stdlib
keywords: sizeof; layout size; byte size
related: concept.binary-layouts stdlib.std::binary::layout_alignment

== NOTES
L is a layout name; resolution replaces the call by the number, so it is
usable wherever a proven Int is. Declared nomethod.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 8
    align 4
    field magic: std::binary::U32 at 0
    field count: std::binary::I16 at 4

[std::binary::layout_length(Header), std::binary::layout_alignment(Header)]
@value [8, 4]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::binary::layout_alignment
title: -
summary: The alignment in bytes of layout L, written std::binary::layout_alignment(L): a compile-time constant.
index: stdlib
keywords: alignof; alignment; layout alignment
related: concept.binary-layouts stdlib.std::binary::layout_length

== NOTES
The `align` the layout declares, folded to a constant at resolution.
Declared nomethod.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::binary

layout Word:
    size 4
    align 4
    field value: std::binary::U32 at 0

std::binary::layout_alignment(Word)
@value 4
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::ffi::c
title: -
summary: The C ABI value universe: target-sized C integer types, and affine C pointers with null, null?, clone, erase and retype.
index: stdlib
keywords: c types; c pointers; ffi; foreign function interface; c abi
related: concept.c-ffi concept.affine

== NOTES
`import std::ffi::c` declares the C integer scalars (std::ffi::c::Char ...
std::ffi::c::ULongLong: integer domains with the compilation target's C
ranges), std::ffi::c::Void, std::ffi::c::Ptr[T] and
std::ffi::c::FnPtr[Fn{...}]. A pointer is affine (binding, passing or
storing it moves it), owns nothing, is never dereferenced, and has no
arithmetic, ordering, equality or conversion to or from an Int. Calling C
itself is `extern c fn` (concept.c-ffi), which runs only on the native
backends.

@topic stdlib.std::ffi::c::null
title: -
summary: The null C pointer of pointee T, written std::ffi::c::null[T]() with the type given explicitly.
index: stdlib
keywords: null pointer; nullptr; empty pointer
related: stdlib.std::ffi::c::null? concept.c-ffi capability.null

== NOTES
T must be imprinted explicitly: std::ffi::c::null() without [T] is the
IMPRINT error, since nothing else establishes T. T is the pointee:
std::ffi::c::Void, a C integer scalar, an opaque struct or a pointer type.
The result is always a data pointer, Ptr[T]:
null[std::ffi::c::FnPtr[Fn{...}]]() is a pointer to a function pointer (a
callback variable's address), not a null function pointer, and no operation
makes a null FnPtr today. Runs on every backend. The result is affine, so it
cannot itself be a program's final value.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::ffi::c

p = std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Int]()
p.null?()
@value true
@end

@topic stdlib.std::ffi::c::null?
title: -
summary: Whether a C pointer (data or function pointer) is null, written p.null?(); it observes the pointer without moving it.
index: stdlib
keywords: is null; null check; nullptr test
related: stdlib.std::ffi::c::null stdlib.std::ffi::c::clone

== NOTES
The one question a pointer answers in Botlish: there is no comparison of two
pointers and no address as an Int. It accepts a data pointer
(std::ffi::c::Ptr[T]) or a function pointer (std::ffi::c::FnPtr[...]), and
the pointer stays usable after the call.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::ffi::c

p = std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Void]()
[p.null?(), p.null?()]
@value [true, true]
@caption Observing does not move the pointer
@end

@topic stdlib.std::ffi::c::callback
title: -
summary: The C function pointer of a `callback c fn`, by name: a std::ffi::c::FnPtr of its declared C signature, whose calls run the function in the Botlish execution environment waiting in a foreign call.
index: stdlib
keywords: function pointer; C callback; FnPtr of a function; callback address
related: concept.c-callbacks error.callback-reference stdlib.std::ffi::c::null?

== NOTES
The argument is the name of a top-level `callback c fn` (CALLBACK-REFERENCE
otherwise), and the operation is only called, never a value. The pointer is
affine, like every C pointer: bind it, then pass it to a C function whose
FnPtr parameter is declared noescape -- C may call it only while such a
call runs (NATIVE CALLBACK otherwise). Native backends only: the Tcl
backends refuse it at run time (NATIVE-ONLY).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::ffi::c

callback c fn decide(value: std::ffi::c::Int) -> std::ffi::c::Int:
    if value > 0:
        return 1
    0

fn pointer():
    cb = std::ffi::c::callback(decide)
    cb.null?()

decide(4)
@value 1
@caption The pointer is made where it is passed to C
@end

@topic stdlib.std::ffi::c::clone
title: -
summary: A second alias of a C pointer (the same address, the same type), written p.clone(): the one way to have two pointers.
index: stdlib
keywords: copy pointer; alias pointer; duplicate pointer
related: stdlib.std::ffi::c::erase concept.affine

== NOTES
Pointers are affine, so `q = p` moves p (using p afterwards is
USE-AFTER-MOVE). clone observes p and returns another pointer to the same
address: no pointee copy, no reference count, no ownership -- C's own
rules decide what the aliases may do.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::ffi::c

p = std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Int]()
q = p.clone()
[p.null?(), q.null?()]
@value [true, true]
@end

@topic stdlib.std::ffi::c::erase
title: -
summary: The same address as a Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void] (C's void *), written p.erase(): the pointer moves into the call.
index: stdlib
keywords: void pointer; cast to void; erase type
related: stdlib.std::ffi::c::retype stdlib.std::ffi::c::clone

== NOTES
Converts a data pointer std::ffi::c::Ptr[T] to
std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]. A function pointer is rejected
(IMPRINT: "data and function pointers are distinct, and nothing converts one
into the other"). The argument is moved (clone first to keep it). There is
no implicit conversion between pointer types: erase and retype are the
explicit ones.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::ffi::c

p = std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Int]()
v = p.erase()
v.null?()
@value true
@end

@topic stdlib.std::ffi::c::retype
title: -
summary: The same address of a Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void] as a Ptr[T], written std::ffi::c::retype[T](p): the pointer moves, and nothing about the pointee is proven.
index: stdlib
keywords: cast pointer; pointer conversion; from void pointer
related: stdlib.std::ffi::c::erase concept.c-ffi

== NOTES
The inverse of erase. Its argument must be a Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]: a
pointer of another pointee is the TYPE error ("a pointer's pointee
identity is never converted implicitly"), so a conversion between two
pointee types goes through erase. T is imprinted explicitly. The call
claims, without proof, that the address holds a T: that is the caller's
C-level responsibility.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::ffi::c

v = std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Void]()
b = std::ffi::c::retype[std::ffi::c::UChar](v)
b.null?()
@value true
@end

