@topic stdlib.std::abi::U8Value
title: -
summary: The Int domain 0..255, the values a std::abi::U8 holds: an integer domain, so its values are ordinary Ints proven in range.
index: stdlib
keywords: u8 range; unsigned 8-bit range; integer domain
related: stdlib.std::abi::U8 concept.integer-domains

@topic stdlib.std::abi::U8
title: -
summary: A unsigned 8-bit ABI integer: a struct whose one field `value` is an Int proven in 0..255; made by std::abi::u8(n).
index: stdlib
keywords: u8; unsigned 8-bit integer; fixed width integer; foreign integer
related: stdlib.std::abi::u8 stdlib.module.std::abi concept.integer-domains

@topic stdlib.std::abi::u8
title: -
summary: The std::abi::U8 representing exactly the Int n, or the declared error std::abi::AbiIntegerBelowRange or std::abi::AbiIntegerAboveRange outside 0..255; never truncates.
index: stdlib
keywords: to u8; convert to unsigned 8-bit; checked conversion; narrow integer
related: stdlib.std::abi::U8 stdlib.std::abi::AbiIntegerBelowRange stdlib.module.std::abi rule.unhandled-errors

== NOTES
Every creator of the family has this shape. A provably in-range argument
(std::abi::u8(42)) needs no handler and a provably out-of-range constant
(std::abi::u8(256)) is KNOWN-ERROR; any other Int must handle or admit both
errors. x.value is the Int back, exactly.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::abi

fn to_u8(n: int) -> int:
    x = std::abi::u8(n):
        on std::abi::AbiIntegerBelowRange:
            return -1
        on std::abi::AbiIntegerAboveRange:
            return 256
    x.value

[std::abi::u8(42).value, to_u8(300), to_u8(-5)]
@value [42, 256, -1]
@end

