related: stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::register64 stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::Register64BelowRange

@topic stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::Register64
title: -
summary: One 64-bit AArch64 register's contents, the transport value of std::linux::abi::aarch64::syscall: no arithmetic, made by register64 or from_*, read by to_int.
index: stdlib
keywords: register; machine word; 64-bit word; syscall register
related: stdlib.std::linux::abi::aarch64::syscall stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::register64 stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::to_int

== NOTES
A nominal struct whose one field `word` holds the register's signed
(two's-complement) reading, a std::abi::aarch64::Register64Word. It is a bit
pattern carried across the kernel boundary, not a machine integer: it
has no arithmetic, ordering or bit operations (r + 5 is the TYPE
error). A Register64 of one architecture is never accepted by the
other's transport. Every conversion runs on every backend; only the
syscall itself and the address bridges are native-only.

@topic stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::register64
title: -
summary: The AArch64 register word whose signed reading is the Int n (-2^63..2^63-1); outside it fails with Register64BelowRange or Register64AboveRange.
index: stdlib
keywords: int to register; register word; syscall number; syscall argument
related: stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::Register64 stdlib.std::abi::aarch64::to_int stdlib.std::linux::abi::aarch64::syscall

== NOTES
The word is n's 64-bit two's-complement bit pattern, so -1 is all ones.
Never truncates. A provably in-range argument (a syscall number such
as 39) needs no handler; an out-of-range constant is KNOWN-ERROR. For
an unsigned value in the upper half of the 64-bit range use from_u64.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::abi::aarch64

r = std::abi::aarch64::register64(-1)
std::abi::aarch64::to_int(r)
@value -1
@end

