@topic stdlib.module.std::web
title: -
summary: URI percent-encoding (std::web::uri_escape_text) and the refinement types std::web::Emailish and std::web::UriQueryValue with their proving predicates.
index: stdlib
keywords: url encoding; percent encoding; uri escape; email validation; web
related: concept.refinements stdlib.module.std::str

== NOTES
Ordinary Botlish. A program that imports web also prints the module's own
ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL findings (its tables of one-character Strings),
which are listed in the warning gate's manifest; they are not about the
importing program.

@topic stdlib.std::web::Emailish
title: -
summary: A String proven to have the shape of an e-mail address by std::web::emailish? (a refinement of str, with a String's representation).
index: stdlib
keywords: email; email address type; validated email
related: stdlib.std::web::emailish? concept.refinements

@topic stdlib.std::web::emailish?
title: -
summary: Whether a String has the shape local@label.tld that stdlib/web.bot accepts as an address, proving it a std::web::Emailish when true.
index: stdlib
keywords: is email; validate email; email check
related: stdlib.std::web::Emailish concept.refinements stdlib.std::str::is_tcl_alnum

== NOTES
The grammar: a local part of letters, digits and . _ % + -; an "@"; one
or more labels of letters, digits and "-", each followed by "."; then a
top-level domain of two or more letters (letters and digits as Tcl 9's
[:alpha:]/[:alnum:] define them). It checks shape only, never that the
address exists. In the true branch of `if std::web::emailish?(v):` v may be
passed where a std::web::Emailish is required.

